erences.py\nlrwxrwxrwx  1 agent agent       16 Nov  7 05:58 global_memory -> ../investigation\n-rw-------  1 agent agent    11907 Nov  7 05:58 global_memory_recent.json\n-rw-------  1 agent agent     4273 Nov  7 05:58 global_memory_summary.md\ndrwxr-xr-x 29 agent agent     4096 Nov  7 06:32 literature\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent    14102 Nov  7 06:50 memory.md\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent     2020 Nov  7 06:36 package_reproduction.py\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent    13373 Nov  7 06:43 paper.aux\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent     8206 Nov  7 06:43 paper.bbl\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent      919 Nov  7 06:43 paper.blg\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent    26973 Nov  2 14:28 paper.bst\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent    17972 Nov  7 06:43 paper.fls\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent    23268 Nov  7 06:43 paper.log\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent     5549 Nov  7 06:43 paper.out\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent   372572 Nov  7 06:43 paper.pdf\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent      576 Nov  7 06:43 paper.pdf.prov.json\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent     8934 Nov  2 14:28 paper.sty\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent    55884 Nov  7 06:42 paper.tex\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent      608 Nov  7 06:43 paper.tex.prov.json\n-rw-------  1 agent agent   934197 Nov  7 06:45 paper_agent_codex_20261107_055810.log\n-rw-------  1 agent agent 35864822 Nov  7 05:58 paper_data.json\ndrwx------  4 agent agent     4096 Nov  7 06:50 paper_diagnostics\n-rw-------  1 agent agent        0 Nov  7 05:58 paper_diagnostics_20261107_055810_27540.log\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent      265 Nov  7 06:26 paper_environment.json\n-rw-------  1 agent agent    12464 Nov  7 06:51 paper_events_20261107_055810.jsonl\n-rw-------  1 agent agent      791 Nov  7 05:58 paper_prep_20261107_055810.json\n-rw-------  1 agent agent    67089 Nov  7 06:10 paper_prompt.md\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent    32135 Nov  7 06:30 paper_strategy.json\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent    31894 Nov  7 06:36 paper_strategy.md\n-rw-------  1 agent agent   488720 Nov  7 06:10 paper_strategy_agent_codex_20261107_055810.log\n-rw-------  1 agent agent    42218 Nov  7 06:04 paper_strategy_prompt.md\n-rw-------  1 agent agent      331 Nov  7 06:10 paper_strategy_resolution.json\n-rw-------  1 agent agent        0 Nov  7 06:51 postdraft_citation_weave_agent_codex_20261107_055810.log\n-rw-------  1 agent agent    76200 Nov  7 06:51 postdraft_citation_weave_prompt.md\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent     3986 Nov  7 06:49 postdraft_reference_query_plan.json\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent     5309 Nov  7 06:49 postdraft_reference_query_plan.md\n-rw-------  1 agent agent   163775 Nov  7 06:50 postdraft_reference_query_plan_agent_codex_20261107_055810.log\n-rw-------  1 agent agent     2559 Nov  7 06:48 postdraft_reference_query_plan_prompt.md\n-rw-------  1 agent agent      265 Nov  7 06:50 postdraft_reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\n-rw-------  1 agent agent      279 Nov  7 06:50 postdraft_reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\n-rw-------  1 agent agent      880 Nov  7 06:51 postdraft_reference_retrieval.log\n-rw-------  1 agent agent     7512 Nov  7 06:32 postdraft_references.bib\n-rw-------  1 agent agent    17080 Nov  7 06:32 postdraft_references_provenance.json\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent     4236 Nov  7 06:32 prepare_sources.py\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent     9822 Nov  7 06:02 reference_query_plan.json\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent    11866 Nov  7 06:02 reference_query_plan.md\n-rw-------  1 agent agent  1347911 Nov  7 06:02 reference_query_plan_agent_codex_20261107_055810.log\n-rw-------  1 agent agent    26903 Nov  7 05:58 reference_query_plan_prompt.md\n-rw-------  1 agent agent      265 Nov  7 06:02 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\n-rw-------  1 agent agent      279 Nov  7 06:02 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\n-rw-------  1 agent agent     3731 Nov  7 06:04 reference_retrieval.log\n-rw-------  1 agent agent     7512 Nov  7 06:51 references.bib\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent      515 Nov  7 06:43 references.bib.prov.json\n-rw-------  1 agent agent     5828 Nov  7 06:51 references.openalex.bib\n-rw-------  1 agent agent     6684 Nov  7 06:51 references.openalex.json\n-rw-------  1 agent agent     7512 Nov  7 06:50 references.pre_postdraft.bib\n-rw-------  1 agent agent    17080 Nov  7 06:50 references.pre_postdraft.json\n-rw-------  1 agent agent    17517 Nov  7 06:51 references_provenance.json\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent      530 Nov  7 06:43 references_provenance.json.prov.json\ndrwxr-xr-x  3 agent agent     4096 Nov  7 06:40 reproduction\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent      716 Nov  7 06:36 requirements-paper.txt\nlrwxrwxrwx  1 agent agent       16 Nov  7 05:58 results -> ../investigation\ndrwxr-xr-x  3 agent agent     4096 Nov  7 06:43 scientific-evidence\ndrwxr-xr-x  2 agent agent     4096 Nov  7 06:40 source_evidence\n-rw-------  1 agent agent      265 Nov  7 06:10 strategy_validation_feedback.json\n-rw-------  1 agent agent      271 Nov  7 06:10 strategy_validation_feedback.md\ndrwxr-xr-x  2 agent agent     4096 Nov  7 06:16 tables\nlrwxrwxrwx  1 agent agent        7 Nov  7 05:58 task -> ../task\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent    12114 Nov  7 06:43 validate_artifacts.py\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent    27335 Nov  7 06:43 validation_report.json\n--- VENUE.md\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.\n--- draft_reference_gaps.md\n# Draft Reference Gap Analysis\n\n**Status:** ready\n\nOne targeted retrieval pass is justified to position the adaptive ILR ellipsoids against joint multivariate conformal-region methods; the remaining citation coverage does not warrant expansion.\n\n## Basis and Scope\n\nReviewed `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, `references_provenance.json`, `claims_grounding.json`, `paper_data.json`, `paper_strategy.json`, and `global_memory_summary.md`. The draft cites all 25 existing bibliography entries, every cited key has a bibliography entry, and the provenance registry covers the same keys. This checks citation presence, not independent full-text verification. The 47 claim-grounding entries distinguish retained numerical evidence, implementation evidence, and literature support. The strategy centers a condition-dependent empirical comparison, not a new conformal guarantee. Global memory was used only as qualitative context.\n\n## Missing Literature\n\nJoint multivariate conformal prediction regions with adaptive residual scale or ellipsoidal geometry.\n\n## Target: related-work-joint-region-comparators\n\n- **Section:** Related Work, existing “Calibration and adaptation” paragraph.\n- **Priority:** high.\n- **Citation intent:** positioning.\n- **Anchor:** “our study instead evaluates joint multivariate membership after residual rescaling”.\n- **Reason:** The paragraph moves from scalar conformalized quantile regression (`Romano2019`) to the paper's joint multivariate construction without directly citing the closest joint-region family. `Lei2018` supports general split-conformal regression; `Stone1974`, `Geisser1975`, and `Barber2021Jackknife` address residual reuse and distinct calibration constructions. The covariance-regularization citations in Method support shape stabilization, not positioning against multivariate conformal regions. Neither the existing bibliography nor its provenance identifies a dedicated joint multivariate conformal-region comparator. This is a reviewer-facing gap because adaptive residual geometry is central to the manuscript's question.\n- **Focused search topics:** “split-conformal multivariate regression ellipsoidal prediction regions residual covariance”; “locally adaptive conformal multivariate prediction regions covariate-dependent residual scaling”.\n- **Bounded follow-up:** Seek 1–3 directly relevant sources and add a brief comparison in this paragraph distinguishing joint-region geometry, adaptation, and separate calibration. Check each source's actual construction before asserting similarity. Do not add experimental baselines, claim superiority over untested methods, or imply that any specific prior method uses the paper's ILR transform or boundary routing. These are retrieval topics, not already verified references.\n\n## No Additional Targets\n\n- Composition geometry and logistic-normal background already have direct citations to `AitchisonShen1980`, `Aitchison1982`, `Egozcue2003`, and `Pawlowsky2011`; their presence does not justify another broad compositional-literature pass.\n- Marginal versus conditional validity, classification distinctions, and covariate-shift corrections already have direct citations in Related Work. The standard calibration argument is also cited in Method.\n- Ridge fitting, covariance regularization, Wilson intervals, and bootstrap uncertainty already have method-specific citations. Implementation details are grounded in source artifacts rather than treated as unsupported literature claims.\n- Reported coverage, volume, controls, and limitations are grounded in retained study evidence. They do not require external citations merely because they are quantitative or unfavorable.\n- Real-data applications, structural zeros, alternative transforms, Hellinger scores, and extra calibration schemes would expand the story beyond the evaluated study and are not retrieval targets.\n\nNo references were retrieved. `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, and `references_provenance.json` remain unchanged. There is no analysis failure (`failure_reason` is empty).\n--- draft_reference_gaps.json\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"summary\": \"One targeted retrieval pass is justified to position the adaptive ILR ellipsoids against joint multivariate conformal-region methods; the remaining citation coverage does not warrant expansion.\",\n  \"missing_literatures\": [\n    \"Joint multivariate conformal prediction regions with adaptive residual scale or ellipsoidal geometry\"\n  ],\n  \"rewrite_targets\": [\n    {\n      \"target_id\": \"related-work-joint-region-comparators\",\n      \"section\": \"Related Work\",\n      \"priority\": \"high\",\n      \"citation_intent\": \"positioning\",\n      \"reason\": \"The Calibration and adaptation paragraph cites scalar conformalized quantile regression before contrasting it with joint multivariate membership after residual rescaling, but supplies no direct citation for the joint multivariate region family closest to the studied adaptive ellipsoids. The existing bibliography and provenance cover general conformal calibration, quantile intervals, cross-validation, and covariance shrinkage, without identifying a dedicated joint multivariate conformal-region comparator. One to three directly relevant sources would address this central positioning gap without changing the empirical comparisons or implying algorithmic novelty.\",\n      \"anchor_text\": \"our study instead evaluates joint multivariate membership after residual rescaling\",\n      \"search_topics\": [\n        \"split-conformal multivariate regression ellipsoidal prediction regions residual covariance\",\n        \"locally adaptive conformal multivariate prediction regions covariate-dependent residual scaling\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
