{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat postdraft_reference_retrieval.log; cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; ls .codex/skills/*/scripts; sed -n '1,105p' paper.tex; cat validation_report.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat postdraft_reference_retrieval.log; cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; ls .codex/skills/*/scripts; sed -n '1,105p' paper.tex; cat validation_report.json\"","aggregated_output":"[paper.sh] reference query plan ready: 2 queries\n[semantic_scholar] search start | query 1/2 | split-conformal multivariate regression ellipsoidal prediction regions residual covariance\n[semantic_scholar] search complete | [------------] | refs 0/25 (25 left) | candidates 0/2 | query 1/2\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [#-----------] | refs 1/25 (24 left) | candidates 1/2 | query 1/2\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [#-----------] | refs 2/25 (23 left) | candidates 2/2 | query 1/2\n[semantic_scholar] search start | query 2/2 | locally adaptive conformal multivariate prediction regions covariate-dependent residual scaling\n[semantic_scholar] search complete | [#-----------] | refs 2/25 (23 left) | candidates 0/0 | query 2/2\n[paper.sh] attempting OpenAlex fallback: Semantic Scholar retrieval did not meet minimum references: 2 < 25\n[paper.sh] OpenAlex contributed 11 references\n---\nname: validate-paper\ndescription: Validate grounded paper artifacts and compile LaTeX PDF with strict fail-fast checks.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nRun final quality gate for the generated paper.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- After `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, and `claims_grounding.json` are written.\n- Before declaring final paper success.\n\n# What to do\n1. Validate sections and citation keys:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_tex_refs.py \\\n  --tex paper.tex \\\n  --bib references.bib\n```\nRequire a substantive embedded Supplementary Information section after the bibliography. It must\nuse `\\\\appendix` and `\\\\section{Supplementary Information}`, contain grounded visible content plus\nat least one subsection, table, or figure, and contain no template placeholder text.\n2. Validate claim mappings, bibliography/provenance consistency, and every `@evidence[[...]]` link in the `scientific-evidence/` bundle:\n```bash\npython ../write-paper/scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis delegates to the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier, which classifies every `@evidence[[...]]` link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`. Any `dangling` link (or an `@evidence[[...]]` token with no `scientific-evidence/` bundle present) is a hard error and must be sent back to the paper-writing agent for repair.\n3. Validate figure manifest and figure readability budget when `figure_manifest.json` is present or `paper_strategy.json` requires figures. The canonical schema is `templates/figure_manifest.schema.json`.\n```bash\npython .claude/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_figures.py \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\n4. Compile:\n```bash\nbash scripts/compile_latex.sh paper.tex\n```\nThe compile script also validates that Supplementary Information is visibly rendered on a page\nafter References with substantive content. Source-only structure checks are not sufficient for\nconditional or literal LaTeX constructs.\nIt records and validates every LaTeX input, restricts BibTeX to the canonical `references.bib`,\nand removes inherited Kpathsea path overrides before compiling.\n5. Require `paper.pdf` to exist.\n6. When `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also fail on visible paper language that leaks pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or similar internal wording.\n7. When the selected format or venue guidance requires real author metadata, also fail on anonymous or generic placeholder author blocks and emit soft warnings when title/abstract/introduction/conclusion lack venue-relevant framing or when the discussion drifts into unrelated application narratives.\n8. Fail hard on any missing grounding link, missing section, missing/empty/misordered Supplementary Information, unresolved citation, bibliography/provenance mismatch, figure-manifest mismatch, density-budget violation, page-limit violation for formats with enforced budgets, standalone-language violation, author-block violation required by format/venue guidance, dangling `@evidence[[...]]` link, or compile error.\n9. Treat the parent orchestrator's validation report as authoritative during repair rounds. Last-body-page fill is rendered geometric vertical occupancy computed from text and graphics bounds, not character count or word density. Do not claim that page validation passed using a character-count proxy. If the canonical validator is unavailable in the agent workspace, make the targeted repair and return control so the parent can compile and revalidate it.\n---\nname: memory-log\ndescription: Maintain a continuous, append-only `./memory.md` log of discoveries, evaluations, failures, and next hypotheses during agent runs.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nKeep durable run memory so idea evolution and program evidence are never lost between steps.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- At run start to initialize memory.\n- After every meaningful step (`idea`, `edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`, `paper`, `draft`).\n- Immediately after every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair.\n\n# What to do\n1. Initialize `./memory.md` if missing with these section headers:\n- `# Memory Log`\n- `## Context`\n- `## Idea Discoveries`\n- `## Program Evaluations`\n- `## Failures / Regressions`\n- `## Next Hypotheses`\n2. Also initialize global files if missing:\n- `./global_memory/memory.md`\n- `./global_memory/memory.jsonl`\n3. Never delete or rewrite prior entries; append only.\n4. For each appended entry, include:\n- timestamp\n- step type\n- change/discovery summary\n- concrete evidence\n- next action\n5. After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append a `Program Evaluations` entry that includes:\n- `program_<idx>`\n- `combined_score`\n- `correct`\n- rubric pass/fail summary\n- `next_recommendation`\n6. For regressions or failed rubrics, append one root-cause hypothesis under `Failures / Regressions`.\n7. Before exit, append final status (`rubric_pass`, `cap_reached`, or task-specific stop reason) and next hypothesis under `Next Hypotheses`.\n8. When command execution is unavailable, use read/edit/write tools to append directly\nto the required markdown and JSONL files. Preserve all existing content. When command\nexecution is available, prefer the helper for deterministic dual-write:\n- script: detect the active agent config directory first, then use `<that-dir>/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py`\n- command template:\n```bash\nMEMORY_HELPER=\"$(find ./.claude ./.codex ./.cursor ./.opencode ./.gemini ./.pi -path '*/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py' -print -quit 2>/dev/null)\"\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" \\\n  --global-memory-md \"./global_memory/memory.md\" \\\n  --global-memory-jsonl \"./global_memory/memory.jsonl\" \\\n  --event-json '{\"step\":\"evaluate\",\"scope\":\"attempt\",\"summary\":\"...\",\"evidence\":\"...\",\"next_action\":\"...\",\"attempt_id\":\"{{ATTEMPT_ID}}\",\"idea_id\":\"{{IDEA_ID}}\",\"program_id\":\"program_2\",\"metrics\":{\"combined_score\":2.54,\"correct\":true}}'\n```\n9. JSONL event schema (required keys):\n- `timestamp` (auto-filled if omitted)\n- `step` (`idea|edit|evaluate|verify|paper|draft|final|conclusions_skip`)\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n**Update gate (the only valid triggers).**\n- (a) cycle **contradicts** an existing sentence → rewrite that sentence.\n- (b) cycle opens a **new category** not yet covered → add a sentence.\n- (c) cycle **flips a Do/Don't** recommendation → flip it.\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n**Pre-flight self-check.** Before invoking the helper, count words (`len(paragraph.split())`) and confirm ≤300, and that the paragraph contains no blank line. Most validation rejections can be avoided here.\n\n**Retry budget — exactly 3 attempts per cycle.** On non-zero exit, read the stderr error, shorten the paragraph (drop the least-decisive sentence, merge two related sentences, or remove qualifiers), and retry. After **3 total attempts** with no success, the update is **discarded** for this cycle: do not retry further, do not write the rejected text anywhere, and append a single memory event with `step=\"conclusions_skip\"` whose `summary` names the last validation error. Underlying findings are not lost — they are already durable in `memory.md`.\n\n**Always log the gate decision.** Inside the regular memory append for the cycle, include `gate=<updated|kept|skipped>; trigger=<a|b|c|d|none>; reason=<one sentence>` in the entry's `summary` or `tags`. Use `skipped` only after the 3-attempt budget is exhausted.\n\n# Example evaluation entry\n```markdown\n### 2026-02-13T16:58:00Z | step=evaluate\n- program: program_3\n- combined_score: 2.54\n- correct: true\n- rubrics: uses_restarts=pass, constraint_optimization=pass, score_threshold=fail\n- evidence: late-stage overlap still visible in stdout trace\n- next_recommendation: raise late-stage overlap penalty and diversify restart seeds\n```\n.codex/skills/memory-log/scripts:\nappend_memory.py\n\n.codex/skills/retrieve-references/scripts:\nopenalex_fetch.py\ns2_fetch.py\nscholarly_fetch.py\n\n.codex/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts:\nscientific_evidence.py\nscientific_evidence.sh\n\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts:\n__pycache__\ncompile_latex.sh\nvalidate_compile_inputs.py\nvalidate_figures.py\nvalidate_rendered_si.py\nvalidate_tex_refs.py\n\n.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/scripts:\nvalidate_claims.py\n\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n% --- end watermark ---\n\\usepackage{paper,times}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,booktabs,longtable,array,graphicx,subcaption}\n\\usepackage{hyperref,url}\n\\title{Adaptive ILR Ellipsoids: Coverage--Volume Trade-offs on the Simplex}\n\\author{Anonymous authors}\n\\newcommand{\\simplex}{\\Delta^{K-1}_{+}}\n\\newcommand{\\R}{\\mathbb{R}}\n\\newcommand{\\clip}{\\operatorname{clip}}\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nPrediction regions for continuous compositions must be evaluated both for joint coverage and for their size on the simplex. We study whether training-adaptive isometric log-ratio (ILR) ellipsoids offer a useful coverage--volume trade-off relative to Euclidean and isotropic ILR balls. The adaptive configuration estimates residual scales and boundary-dependent shapes using training data, while an independent split-conformal calibration determines the region threshold. Across five held-out seeds and six synthetic conditions, heteroskedastic logistic-normal responses yield mean coverage 0.9044 and simplex-volume fraction 0.1476, compared with 0.9030 and 0.3336 for Euclidean balls. The benefit is not uniform: boundary-concentrated Dirichlet responses require volume fraction 0.9759, while covariate-shifted coverage falls to 0.8633 against a 0.90 target. Descriptive sign-stratum coverage also remains uneven. These findings support condition-dependent evaluation of region geometry, not universal superiority or conditional validity. Common-measure integration and separate uncertainty summaries expose failures that a single aggregate objective would obscure.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json]]\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nCompositional responses describe how a whole is distributed among its parts: each component is nonnegative and the components sum to one. Uncertainty about an observed composition is therefore a joint, constrained prediction problem. An interval for every component does not by itself specify a calibrated joint region, and a region that covers almost the entire simplex can be reliable yet uninformative. This distinction matters even when the point predictor is accurate. We consider continuous composition-valued responses, not the discrete label of a classifier or an unobserved population probability vector.\n\nConformal prediction separates the quality of a fitted predictor from the calibration of its prediction region \\citep{Vovk2005,Papadopoulos2002,Angelopoulos2021}. Under exchangeability, a calibration sample supplies a finite-sample marginal coverage guarantee for a frozen score. That guarantee does not choose the score geometry, ensure small regions, or establish coverage at particular covariate values. Compositional geometry introduces an additional choice: distances in raw proportion coordinates and distances in log-ratio coordinates need not describe similarly sized subsets of the simplex \\citep{Aitchison1982,Egozcue2003}. Comparing calibrated radii across these spaces is consequently insufficient.\n\nOur question is whether a training-adaptive ILR residual geometry yields smaller prediction regions at comparable observed joint coverage, and which conditions defeat that trade-off. We study a specific configuration that combines held-out training residuals, covariate-dependent diagonal scales, and training-defined boundary groups. Its threshold is calibrated independently, using the same split-conformal rule as two simpler reference geometries. All regions are evaluated by membership and by the same normalized simplex-volume measure. This makes the comparison meaningful even when their native coordinate systems differ.\n\nThe empirical contribution is deliberately scoped. We provide a matched evaluation of the three region families across homoskedastic, heteroskedastic, boundary-concentrated, and shifted synthetic responses, together with information-removal controls. We expose the distinction between integration error, finite-test uncertainty, and between-seed dispersion. We also retain a direct comparison of alternative adaptive training configurations, while explicitly declining to attribute bundled changes to cross-fitting alone. The central finding is a useful heteroskedastic trade-off accompanied by substantial boundary inefficiency and persistent shifted undercoverage; it is not a new universal conformal guarantee.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/adaptive.py#L90-L131]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/geometry_calibration_overview.png}\n\\caption{Study overview. Training data determine the location and geometry; independent calibration determines a single threshold. Euclidean balls, ILR balls, and adaptive ILR ellipsoids are compared by joint response coverage and a common simplex-volume measure. Boundary groups adapt shape, not calibration. Any simplex icon is conceptual, not a measured response cloud.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Composition-valued prediction.}\nLogistic-normal models describe continuous distributions on the simplex through transformed Gaussian coordinates \\citep{AitchisonShen1980}. Log-ratio analysis addresses the dependence imposed by closure; ILR coordinates provide an orthonormal representation of its contrast space \\citep{Aitchison1982,Egozcue2003,Pawlowsky2011}. We use this representation as a working geometry, not as evidence that all simplex distributions become well approximated by ellipsoids. In particular, strictly positive Dirichlet responses can lie close to the boundary, where log ratios are highly sensitive to small components.\n\n\\paragraph{Calibration and adaptation.}\nSplit-conformal regression allows a flexible fitted score while retaining marginal calibration \\citep{Lei2018}. Conformalized quantile regression adapts interval width to heteroskedasticity \\citep{Romano2019}; our study instead evaluates joint multivariate membership after residual rescaling. Cross-validation and predictive sample reuse motivate held-out residual estimation \\citep{Stone1974,Geisser1975}, but training residual cross-fitting is not itself a conformal procedure. In particular, the present construction is not jackknife+: that method combines leave-one-out predictions and residuals in its region construction \\citep{Barber2021Jackknife}. Our separate calibration sample remains essential.\n\n\\paragraph{Coverage targets and evaluation.}\nConditional validity is stronger than marginal validity, and distribution-free conditional inference faces fundamental limitations \\citep{Vovk2012,Barber2021Limits}. Label-adaptive and class-conditional classification methods address distinct targets from coverage of an observed continuous composition \\citep{Romano2020,Ding2023}. Weighted conformal methods and extensions beyond exchangeability address distribution shift with additional structure \\citep{Tibshirani2019,Barber2023}; we do not implement those corrections. Instead, shift is an explicit stress test. Finally, our coverage--volume objective is not a proper scoring rule for a predictive distribution \\citep{Gneiting2007}. Reporting its components separately is necessary to distinguish empirical compactness from reliability.\n\n\\section{Method}\n\\subsection{Problem Setup and Design Rationale}\nLet $X\\in\\R^5$ and $Y\\in\\simplex=\\{y\\in(0,1)^K:\\sum_j y_j=1\\}$. The target is a region $C_\\alpha(X)$ containing a future observed composition with probability at least $1-\\alpha$, marginally under an exchangeable calibration/test law. The evaluated target is $\\alpha=0.1$. We study joint membership of the entire response vector; neither componentwise intervals nor coverage of a latent distribution is the estimand. The empirical trade-off is test coverage versus normalized volume under the uniform distribution on the simplex.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L34-L45]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n\nThe working hypothesis is that a pooled residual norm wastes volume when residual scales vary predictably with $X$. Training-derived scale and shape adjustments may align the region with this heterogeneity before calibration. Boundary-dependent shapes are a limited proxy for variation in residual geometry, not a guarantee of local validity. The countervailing hypothesis is that log-ratio tail behavior or covariate shift can defeat compactness or coverage despite this adaptation. These hypotheses concern end-to-end configurations: the experiment does not isolate the causal contribution of every fitted component.\n\n\\subsection{Reference Geometries}\nChoose $B\\in\\R^{K\\times(K-1)}$ with $B^TB=I$ and $B^T\\mathbf{1}=0$. Write $z(y)=B^T\\log y$ and $h(z)=\\operatorname{softmax}(Bz)$. All location models use a quadratic design $\\phi(x)$ containing an intercept, each input, its square, and distinct pairwise products. Ridge fits use penalty $0.01$, including the intercept \\citep{HoerlKennard1970}. The Euclidean reference fits responses directly, clips fitted components below $10^{-12}$, renormalizes, and uses $s_E(x,y)=\\|y-\\widehat p(x)\\|_2$. The ILR reference fits $z(Y)$ and uses $s_I(x,y)=\\|z(y)-\\widehat m_I(x)\\|_2$. Both references fit locations on the first three quarters of the available training rows. Their calibrated sets are respectively simplex-intersected Euclidean balls and inverse-ILR images of isotropic balls.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L624-L680]]\n\n\\subsection{Training-Adaptive ILR Residual Geometry}\nThe studied adaptive configuration partitions training rows deterministically into four folds by row index modulo four. Each row receives a residual $r_i=z(y_i)-\\widehat m^{(-f(i))}(x_i)$ from a ridge location fit excluding its fold. A final location $\\widehat m$ is refitted on all training rows. For each ILR coordinate, a second ridge regression with penalty $5$ fits $\\log(r_{ij}^2+0.02)$ on $\\phi(x_i)$. With fitted value $v_j(x)$, define\n\\begin{equation}\n D(x)=\\operatorname{diag}(\\sigma_j(x)),\\qquad\n \\sigma_j(x)=\\exp\\!\\left\\{\\tfrac12\\clip(v_j(x),-6,5)\\right\\}.\n \\label{eq:scale}\n\\end{equation}\nThe standardized residual vector $u_i=D(x_i)^{-1}r_i$ is clipped coordinatewise to $[-1000,1000]$. These safeguards are fixed numerical choices, not independently optimized or ablated findings.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/adaptive.py#L90-L123]]\n\nBoundary proximity is summarized by $b(x)=\\min_j h(\\widehat m(x))_j$. Training predictions define its lower and upper tertiles; right-sided threshold assignment gives a group $g(x)$. For dimension $d=K-1$, the fitted second-moment shapes are\n\\begin{equation}\n S_0=\\frac{\\sum_i u_i u_i^T+8I_d}{n_{\\rm tr}+8},\\qquad\n S_g=\\frac{\\sum_{i:g(x_i)=g}u_i u_i^T+32S_0}{n_g+32}.\n \\label{eq:shape}\n\\end{equation}\nLet $H_gH_g^T=S_g$ be its Cholesky factor and $L(x)=D(x)H_{g(x)}$. The adaptive score is\n\\begin{equation}\n s_A(x,y)=\\left\\|L(x)^{-1}\\{z(y)-\\widehat m(x)\\}\\right\\|_2.\n \\label{eq:score}\n\\end{equation}\nThese are uncentered residual second moments, not unbiased sample covariances. Shrinkage toward a positive target stabilizes the shapes, in the broad spirit of covariance regularization \\citep{SchaferStrimmer2005,Chen2010}; this is not an implementation of either cited estimator. All group boundaries, scales, and factors are frozen before calibration. Only the residual geometry is group-dependent: no groupwise conformal quantiles are fitted.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/adaptive.py#L116-L131]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/adaptive.py#L190-L198]]\n\n\\subsection{Independent Calibration and Guarantee Scope}\nFor any one of the frozen scores, sort its $n_{\\rm cal}$ calibration values and select rank $k=\\lceil(n_{\\rm cal}+1)(1-\\alpha)\\rceil$. Use $q=+\\infty$ if $k>n_{\\rm cal}$; otherwise use the $k$th value. The region is $C_\\alpha(x)=\\{y\\in\\simplex:s(x,y)\\le q\\}$. Conditional on a fixed training fit, exchangeability of the calibration and future scores gives the standard rank argument:\n\\begin{equation}\n \\Pr\\{Y_{\\rm new}\\in C_\\alpha(X_{\\rm new})\\}\\ge 1-\\alpha.\n \\label{eq:coverage}\n\\end{equation}\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready_for_orchestrator_render\",\n  \"checked_at\": \"2026-11-07T06:43:36.154347+00:00\",\n  \"checks\": {\n    \"tex_references\": {\n      \"returncode\": 0,\n      \"stdout\": \"Validated sections and 25 citation key references.\\n\",\n      \"stderr\": \"\"\n    },\n    \"latex_initial\": {\n      \"returncode\": 0,\n      \"stdout\": \"This is pdfTeX, Version 3.141592653-2.6-1.40.26 (TeX Live 2025/dev/Debian) (preloaded format=pdflatex)\\nentering extended mode\\n(./paper.tex\\nLaTeX2e <2024-11-01> patch level 2\\nL3 programming layer <2025-01-18>\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/base/article.cls\\nDocument Class: article 2024/06/29 v1.4n Standard LaTeX document class\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/base/size10.clo))\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/xcolor/xcolor.sty\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/graphics-cfg/color.cfg)\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/graphics-def/pdftex.def)\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/graphics/mathcolor.ltx))\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/l3packages/xfp/xfp.sty\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/l3kernel/expl3.sty\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/l3backend/l3backend-pdftex.def)))\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/draftwatermark/draftwatermark.sty\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/kvoptions/kvoptions.sty\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/graphics/keyval.sty)\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/generic/ltxcmds/ltxcmds.sty)\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/kvsetkeys/kvsetkeys.sty))\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/graphics/graphicx.sty\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/graphics/graphics.sty\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/graphics/trig.sty)\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/graphics-cfg/graphics.cfg))))\\n(./paper.sty (/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/eso-pic/eso-pic.sty)\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/fancyhdr/fancyhdr.sty)\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/natbib/natbib.sty))\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/psnfss/times.sty)\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/amsmath/amsmath.sty\\nFor additional information on amsmath, use the `?' option.\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/amsmath/amstext.sty\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/amsmath/amsgen.sty))\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/amsmath/amsbsy.sty)\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/amsmath/amsopn.sty))\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/amsfonts/amssymb.sty\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/amsfonts/amsfonts.sty))\\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/booktab