{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom bs4 import BeautifulSoup\ns=BeautifulSoup(open('/tmp/ccle.html').read(),'html.parser')\nprint(s.title.get_text()); print('AUTHORS:',[x.get_text(' ',strip=True) for x in s.select('.ltx_personname')]); print('ABSTRACT:',[x.get_text(' ',strip=True) for x in s.select('.ltx_abstract')]);\nfor x in s.find_all(['h2','h3']): print(x.get('id'), x.get_text(' ',strip=True))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"Adaptive inference with random ellipsoids through Conformal Conditional Linear Expectation\nAUTHORS: ['Iain Henderson', 'Adrien Mazoyer', 'Fabrice Gamboa']\nABSTRACT: ['Abstract We propose two new conformity scores for conformal prediction, in a general multivariate regression framework. The underlying score functions are based on a covariance analysis of the residuals and the input points.\\nWe give theoretical guarantees on the prediction sets, which consist in explicit ellipsoids. We study the asymptotic properties of the ellipsoids, and show that their volume is reduced compared to that of classic balls, under ellipticity assumptions. Finally, we illustrate the effectiveness of all our results on an in-depth numerical study, including heavy-tailed as well as non-elliptical distributions.']\nNone 1 Introduction\nNone 2 Background for Split Conformal Inference\nNone 3 The Mahalanobis score and main results\nNone 3.1 Notations\nNone 3.2 Joint Mahalanobis score\nNone 3.3 The first ellipsoid ℰ α n \\mathcal{E}_{\\alpha}^{n} : main theorem\nNone 3.4 From the score matrix 𝐒 ⁡ ( z ) \\mathbf{S}(z) to the ellipsoid ℰ α n \\mathcal{E}_{\\alpha}^{n}\nNone 3.5 The adjusted Mahalanobis score and the second ellipsoid ℱ α n \\mathcal{F}_{\\alpha}^{n}\nNone 4 Asymptotic analysis\nNone 4.1 The first asymptotic ellipsoid ℰ α ∞ \\mathcal{E}_{\\alpha}^{\\infty}\nNone 4.2 The second asymptotic ellipsoid ℱ α ∞ \\mathcal{F}_{\\alpha}^{\\infty}\nNone 4.3 Comparison with the norm residual score\nNone 5 Numerical application\nNone 5.1 Gaussian data\nNone 5.2 Cauchy data\nNone 5.3 Non elliptical data : the inverse Dirichlet distribution\nNone 6 Conclusion\nNone Appendix A Additional remarks\nNone A.1 Interpretations of the score vector diag ​ ( 𝐒 ​ ( z ) ) \\text{diag}(\\mathbf{S}(z))\nNone A.2 Different assumptions for Proposition 3.4\nNone A.3 Infinite variance case\nNone A.4 Non asymptotic analysis in the Gaussian case\nNone Appendix B Technical lemmas\nNone Appendix C Proofs\nNone References\nNone Instructions for reporting errors\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
