{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom bs4 import BeautifulSoup\ns=BeautifulSoup(open('/tmp/ccle.html').read(),'html.parser')\nfor section in s.select('section'):\n heading=section.find(['h2','h3'],recursive=False)\n if heading and heading.get_text(' ',strip=True).startswith(('2 Background','3.1 Notations','3.2 Joint','3.3 The first','3.5 The adjusted')):\n  for math in section.find_all('math'): math.replace_with(' '+math.get('alttext','[math]')+' ')\n  for link in section.select('.ltx_ref'): link.replace_with('[reference]')\n  print(section.get('id'),section.get_text(' ',strip=True))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom bs4 import BeautifulSoup\ns=BeautifulSoup(open('/tmp/ccle.html').read(),'html.parser')\nfor section in s.select('section'):\n heading=section.find(['h2','h3'],recursive=False)\n if heading and heading.get_text(' ',strip=True).startswith(('2 Background','3.1 Notations','3.2 Joint','3.3 The first','3.5 The adjusted')):\n  for math in section.find_all('math'): math.replace_with(' '+math.get('alttext','[math]')+' ')\n  for link in section.select('.ltx_ref'): link.replace_with('[reference]')\n  print(section.get('id'),section.get_text(' ',strip=True))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"S2 2 Background for Split Conformal Inference Let (U_{i})_{i=1,\\ldots,n+1} be (n+1) exchangeable vectors of random variables, with U_{i}=(X_{i},Y_{i})\\in\\mathcal{U}=\\mathcal{X}\\times\\mathcal{Y} .\nThis means that the joint distribution of (U_{i})_{i=1,\\ldots,n+1} is invariant by any permutation of the symmetric group of order (n+1) . For example, an i.i.d. sample satisfies this assumption. In our framework we assume that (U_{i})_{i=1,\\ldots,n} , called the calibration set (or calibration sample), has been observed and that we get X_{n+1} . We assume further that we have at hand a predictor \\widehat{f}(X_{n+1}) of Y_{n+1} . In this paper, for the sake of simplicity we assume that this predictor is deterministic. For example, it had been built using a sample independent of (U_{i})_{i=1,\\ldots,n+1} and so we are working conditionally to the training sample (this is the main assumption of split CI, in contrast with full CI where \\widehat{f} is retrained for each new sample point).\nThe aim is to provide a prediction set for Y_{n+1} .\nMore precisely, as discussed in the Introduction, our objective is to construct a conservatively valid prediction set for Y_{n+1} with a given level of confidence. Hence, we aim to build a set function C_{\\alpha}^{n} defined on (\\mathcal{X}\\times\\mathcal{Y})^{n}\\times\\mathcal{X} that will return a measurable subset of \\mathcal{Y} for Y_{n+1} such that \\mathbb{P}\\left(Y_{n+1}\\in C_{\\alpha}^{n}\\left((X_{i},Y_{i})_{i=1\\dots n};X_{n+1}\\right)\\right)\\geq 1-\\alpha\\,. (3) Notice that in the above probability we have integrated over (X_{i},Y_{i})_{i=1\\dots n+1} .\nThe construction of C_{\\alpha}^{n} stands on the notion of conformity. To begin with, we define a nonconformity score. It consists in a map S from \\mathcal{U}^{n}\\times\\mathcal{U} to \\mathbb{R} , symmetric in its first n arguments. The empirical distribution built from (S(U_{1},\\ldots,U_{n};U_{j}))_{j=1,\\ldots,n} will be the main tool to quantify how conformal to U_{1},\\ldots,U_{n} , u\\in\\mathcal{U} is.\nArchetypal scores in the usual regression framework with \\mathcal{U}=\\mathbb{R}^{k}\\times\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell} are related to the magnitude of the empirical residual, the most popular example being S((X_{1},Y_{1}),\\dots,(X_{n},Y_{n}),(x,y))=\\|y-\\widehat{f}(x)\\|, (4) where \\|\\cdot\\| is the Euclidean norm of \\mathbb{R}^{\\ell} .\nNotice that in this last example the score function only depends on u=(x,y) but not on the calibration sample (U_{1},\\ldots,U_{n}) . This will not be the case in the framework that we are developing in this paper (see the next section). Let \\alpha\\in(1/(n+1),1) . For the sake of conciseness, we will use the notation S(x,y)\\coloneqq S((X_{1},Y_{1}),\\dots,(X_{n},Y_{n});(x,y)) . For i=1,\\ldots,n+1 , we will also write S_{i}\\coloneqq S(X_{i},Y_{i}) .\nFollowing for example [ [reference] ] , the conformalized prediction set for Y_{n+1} when observing X_{n+1} is then C_{\\alpha}^{n}(X_{n+1})\\equiv C_{\\alpha}^{n}((X_{1},Y_{1})\\dots(X_{n},Y_{n});X_{n+1})=\\left\\{y\\in\\mathcal{Y}\\;{:}\\;S(X_{n+1},y)\\leq S_{(n_{\\alpha})}\\right\\}. (5) Here, n_{\\alpha}\\coloneqq\\left\\lceil(1-\\alpha)(n+1)\\right\\rceil and S_{(1)}\\leq\\dots\\leq S_{(n)} denotes the order statistics associated to (S_{i})_{i=1\\dots n} . By construction, the above set C_{\\alpha}^{n}(X_{n+1}) satisfies \\displaystyle\\mathbb{P}\\left(Y_{n+1}\\in C_{\\alpha}^{n}(X_{n+1})\\right)\\geq 1-\\alpha. (6) The key property ensuring that equation ( [reference] ) holds is that (S_{i})_{i=1,\\ldots,n+1} is exchangeable. Moreover, if the nonconformity scores (S_{i})_{i=1\\dots n} have a continuous joint distribution, then we have the following upper bound, \\displaystyle\\mathbb{P}\\left(Y_{n+1}\\in C_{\\alpha}^{n}(X_{n+1})\\right)\\leq 1-\\alpha+\\frac{1}{n+1}\\,. (7) Returning to the regression framework and considering the residual score ( [reference] ), the prediction set for Y_{n+1} given the covariable X_{n+1} is \\displaystyle C_{\\alpha}^{n}(X_{n+1})=B\\left(\\widehat{f}(X_{n+1}),S_{(n_{\\alpha})}\\right)\\,, i.e. the ball with center \\widehat{f}(X_{n+1}) and radius S_{(n_{\\alpha})} .\nS3.SS1 3.1 Notations We will assume that \\mathcal{X}=\\mathbb{R}^{k} and \\mathcal{Y}=\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell} , ( \\ell,k\\in\\mathbb{N} ).\nFor any positive integer n_{1} and n_{2} , \\mathcal{M}_{n_{1},n_{2}} denotes the set of all n_{1}\\times n_{2} real matrices. For, i=1\\ldots,n , e_{i} denotes the i\\textsuperscript{th} element of the canonical basis of \\mathbb{R}^{n} . Set p=k+\\ell , for v\\in\\mathbb{R}^{p}\\setminus\\{0\\} , \\pi^{\\perp}_{v} denotes the orthogonal projector onto \\text{Span}(v)^{\\perp} . In others words, \\pi^{\\perp}_{v}=\\mathbf{I}_{p}-\\|v\\|^{-2}vv^{\\top} . \\mathbbm{1} denotes the vector compounded by ones on all its components (its dimension will be implicit from the context). For any \\alpha\\in(0,1) , we set n_{\\alpha}\\coloneqq\\lceil(1-\\alpha)(n+1)\\rceil . All non-column matrices are denoted with bold letters. For any block matrix \\mathbf{M}=\\bigl(\\begin{smallmatrix}\\mathbf{A}&\\mathbf{B}\\\\\n\\mathbf{C}&\\mathbf{D}\\end{smallmatrix}\\bigr) , we will denote the Schur complement (whenever it exists ) by \\mathbf{M}/\\mathbf{A}\\coloneqq\\mathbf{D}-\\mathbf{C}\\mathbf{A}^{-1}\\mathbf{B} . The abbreviation SLLN stands for the strong law of large numbers. Given A\\in\\mathcal{M}_{n_{1},n_{2}} (n_{1},n_{2}\\geq 1) , its Frobenius norm is denoted as \\|A\\|_{F}^{2}\\coloneqq\\mathrm{Tr}(AA^{\\top}) . Given A,B\\in\\mathcal{M}_{n,n} two symmetric matrices, A\\preccurlyeq B means that B-A is nonnegative definite. Let \\xi be real random variable and \\alpha\\in(0,1) , q_{1-\\alpha}(\\xi) denotes its quantile of order 1-\\alpha , i.e. q_{1-\\alpha}(\\xi)=\\inf\\{r\\in\\mathbb{R}\\,{:}\\,\\mathbb{P}(\\xi>r)\\leq\\alpha\\} . Recall also that we have a predictor \\widehat{f} independent of the calibration sample and that we denote the residual by R\\coloneqq Y-\\widehat{Y},\\,\\widehat{Y}=\\widehat{f}(X) .\nFor a square integrable random vector \\xi , \\mu_{\\xi} and \\mathbf{\\Sigma}_{\\xi} denote its mean vector and covariance matrix, respectively. We abbreviate “almost surely” as “a.s.”. \\xlongrightarrow{\\mathbb{P}} stands for convergence in probability, \\stackrel{{\\scriptstyle\\mathrm{d}}}{{=}} and \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{d}} stand for equality and convergence in distribution, respectively.\nTo finish, we represent all finite-dimensional vectors as column matrices.\nS3.SS2 3.2 Joint Mahalanobis score As discussed in the previous section, a useful score used in CI is the (squared) norm of the residual.\nIn this paper, we will consider a natural generalization of the previous score by considering some statistical evaluation of (R-\\mu_{R})^{\\top}\\mathbf{\\Sigma}_{R}^{-1}(R-\\mu_{R}) (assuming that everything here is well defined). Before explaining how we will estimate and handle the previous quantity, let it first bulk up\nby including X in the score. For this purpose, let V\\coloneqq(X^{\\top}\\ R^{\\top})^{\\top}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{p} and set \\displaystyle\\mathcal{S}=(V-\\mu_{V})^{\\top}\\mathbf{\\Sigma}^{-1}_{V}(V-\\mu_{V}). (8) As before, we assume here that everything is well defined. The intuition behind this score is to work with standardized quantities. One may recognize in ( [reference] ) the so-called Mahalanobis quadratic form, which in turn defines the Mahalanobis metric. This metric is classical in multivariate analysis and widely used for example for classification tasks (see [ [reference] ] ).\nThe idea of including the covariate X in the score is inspired by the CI framework for time series, where X can contain the k previous residuals (see e.g. [ [reference] ] , Section 4.2, or the end of this subsection) : the joint distribution (X^{\\top}R^{\\top})^{\\top} is relevant for predicting the future \\ell residuals (i.e. R ). In the context of CI, the position and covariance parameters of V are of course unknown or even undefined, and it is natural to replace them with their empirical estimators.\nNevertheless, in order to preserve exchangeability and so the finite sample theoretical guarantees provided by the classical CI procedure, one has to process such empirical estimators in an exchangeable fashion. Of course, another way to proceed would be to estimate these parameters separately, similarly to the pre-trained predictor, leading to a double split procedure. The first option is more challenging and this is our approach here. To begin with,\nwe now introduce precisely our working score. We first set V_{i}=\\begin{pmatrix}X_{i}\\\\\nR_{i}\\end{pmatrix}\\;(i=1,\\ldots,n)\\mbox{ and, for }z\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell},\\;V_{n+1}(z)=\\begin{pmatrix}X_{n+1}\\\\\nz\\end{pmatrix}. (9) Here, the variable z=y-\\widehat{f}(X_{n+1}) (resp. y ) is used to locate the most likely potential residuals (resp. predictions) in the CI machinery.\nWe store the observation vectors V_{i},\\;i=1\\ldots,n and the guess V_{n+1}(z) in a matrix \\mathbf{V}(z)\\in\\mathcal{M}_{n+1,p} whose first n row are the V_{i}^{\\top},i=1,\\ldots,n and the last one is V_{n+1}^{\\top}(z) .\nFollowing equation ( [reference] ), we decompose \\mathbf{V}(z) as \\mathbf{V}(z)=(\\mathbf{X}\\ \\mathbf{R}(z)) with \\mathbf{X}\\in\\mathcal{M}_{n+1,k} and \\mathbf{R}(z)\\in\\mathcal{M}_{n+1,\\ell} . The empirically centered counterpart of \\mathbf{V}(z) is \\mathbf{W}(z)=\\pi^{\\perp}_{\\mathbbm{1}}\\mathbf{V}(z) .\nWe further compute the ridge empirical covariance matrix \\widehat{\\mathbf{\\Sigma}}_{\\lambda}(z)\\in\\mathcal{M}_{p,p} , \\displaystyle\\widehat{\\mathbf{\\Sigma}}_{\\lambda}(z)=\\frac{1}{n}\\mathbf{W}(z)^{\\top}\\mathbf{W}(z)+\\lambda\\mathbf{I}. (10) Here, \\lambda>0 is the ridge parameter.\nWith all these supplementary notations, we are now able to define the score S_{i}(z)\\;(1\\leq i\\leq n+1) as the square of the empirical Mahanalobis norm of W_{i}(z) (the i^{\\mathrm{th}} row of \\mathbf{W}(z) written in column). Namely, we have \\displaystyle S_{i}(z)=\\|\\widehat{\\mathbf{\\Sigma}}_{\\lambda}^{-1/2}(z)W_{i}(z)\\|^{2}=W_{i}(z)^{\\top}\\widehat{\\mathbf{\\Sigma}}_{\\lambda}^{-1}(z)W_{i}(z). (11) It is readily seen that S_{i}(z) is the i\\textsuperscript{th} diagonal element of the following matrix, \\displaystyle\\mathbf{S}(z) \\displaystyle=\\mathbf{W}(z)\\bigg(\\frac{1}{n}\\mathbf{W}(z)^{\\top}\\mathbf{W}(z)+\\lambda\\mathbf{I}\\bigg)^{-1}\\mathbf{W}(z)^{\\top} \\displaystyle=n\\mathbf{W}(z)\\Big(\\mathbf{W}(z)^{\\top}\\mathbf{W}(z)+n\\lambda\\mathbf{I}\\Big)^{-1}\\mathbf{W}(z)^{\\top}. (12) Remarkably, and contrarily to the norm residual score, the first n scores (S_{1}(z),\\ldots,S_{n}(z)) also depend on z . This property induces a number of technical difficulties which are dealt with in Section [reference] . To alleviate the notations we will sometimes omit the dependence in z in the matrix valued functions. First intuition on the confidence regions associated to S(z) Before continuing with the main results, let us consider the Gaussian case (X^{\\top}R^{\\top})^{\\top}\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,\\Sigma) . Anticipating Section [reference] , we replace the ridge empirical covariance with \\Sigma and set \\lambda=0 . Then, setting T(z)=z-\\Sigma_{21}\\Sigma_{11}^{-1}X with z=y-\\widehat{f}(X) and using the block inversion lemma (equation ( [reference] )), our score amounts to \\displaystyle S(z) \\displaystyle=\\begin{pmatrix}X\\\\\nz\\end{pmatrix}^{\\top}\\mathbf{\\Sigma}^{-1}\\begin{pmatrix}X\\\\\nz\\end{pmatrix}=X^{\\top}(\\mathbf{\\Sigma}^{11})^{-1}X+T(z)^{\\top}(\\mathbf{\\Sigma}/\\mathbf{\\Sigma}^{11})^{-1}T(z). (13) Assume also that the quantile of order 1-\\alpha of S(R) (denoted by q_{1-\\alpha}^{\\mathcal{E}} ) is known, so that we can replace the empirical quantile usually computed in CI by its theoretical value q_{1-\\alpha}^{\\mathcal{E}} .\nThe confidence region becomes \\displaystyle\\mathcal{E}_{\\alpha}^{\\infty} \\displaystyle=\\{z:X^{\\top}(\\mathbf{\\Sigma}^{11})^{-1}X+T(z)^{\\top}(\\mathbf{\\Sigma}/\\mathbf{\\Sigma}^{11})^{-1}T(z)\\leq q_{1-\\alpha}^{\\mathcal{E}}\\}. (14) Under our Gaussianity assumption, q_{1-\\alpha}^{\\mathcal{E}}=q_{1-\\alpha}(\\chi^{2}(p)) . The ellipsoid \\mathcal{E}_{\\alpha}^{\\infty} has two important features : it is centered on the conditional expectation \\mathbb{E}[R|X]=\\Sigma_{21}\\Sigma_{11}^{-1}X and its matrix is equal to the conditional covariance \\mathrm{Cov}(R|X)=\\mathbf{\\Sigma}/\\mathbf{\\Sigma}^{11} . However, \nwe can see in this simple case that \\mathcal{E}_{\\alpha}^{\\infty}=\\varnothing\\iff X^{\\top}(\\mathbf{\\Sigma}^{11})^{-1}X\\geq q_{1-\\alpha}^{\\mathcal{E}} . This observation suggests the introduction of S^{\\prime}(z) , an adjusted version of the score S(z) , defined as S^{\\prime}(z)=S(z)-X^{\\top}(\\mathbf{\\Sigma}^{11})^{-1}X=T(z)^{\\top}(\\mathbf{\\Sigma}/\\mathbf{\\Sigma}^{11})^{-1}T(z). (15) The score S^{\\prime} is studied in Section [reference] .\nThis new score leads to a second confidence region \\mathcal{F}_{\\alpha}^{\\infty}=\\{z:T(z)^{\\top}(\\mathbf{\\Sigma}/\\mathbf{\\Sigma}^{11})^{-1}T(z)\\leq q_{1-\\alpha}^{\\mathcal{F}}\\}, (16) where q_{1-\\alpha}^{\\mathcal{F}}=q_{1-\\alpha}(\\chi^{2}(\\ell)) in the Gaussian case. Contrarily to \\mathcal{E}_{\\alpha}^{\\infty} , this region is never empty as \\Sigma_{21}\\Sigma_{11}^{-1}X\\in\\mathcal{F}_{\\alpha}^{\\infty} . In return, its finite sample counterpart \\mathcal{F}_{\\alpha}^{n} can equal to \\mathbb{R}^{\\ell} (Section [reference] ).\nObserve finally that when working with the score matrix ( [reference] ),\nit is not obvious anymore that the prediction region built with the score matrix ( [reference] ) is an ellipsoid, although we expect to obtain a prediction set similar to \\mathcal{E}_{\\alpha}^{\\infty} . Computation of the score Let us comment the effective computation of equation ( [reference] ) before stating the upcoming Theorem [reference] .\nThe evaluation of \\mathbf{S}(z) in ( [reference] ) is not directly tractable: for each candidate z , both a matrix inversion and matrix products have to be performed. Fortunately, it turns out that explicit inversion tricks can be used to overcome this first issue. First, notice that the matrix \\mathbf{V}(z) is a linear function of z . More explicitly, we may write \\mathbf{V}(z)=\\mathbf{V}(0)+\\mathbf{A}(z) , where \\mathbf{A}(z) is the rank one matrix given by \\displaystyle\\mathbf{A}(z)=e_{n+1}\\bigg(\\sum_{s=1}^{\\ell}z_{s}e_{k+s}\\bigg)^{\\top}=e_{n+1}(\\mathbf{L}z)^{\\top}, (17) where \\mathbf{L}\\in\\mathcal{M}_{p,\\ell} is the matrix such that \\mathbf{L}^{\\top}=(\\mathbf{0}_{\\ell,k}\\ \\vline\\ \\mathbf{I}_{\\ell}) . Thus, \\displaystyle\\mathbf{W}(z)=\\pi^{\\perp}_{\\mathbbm{1}}\\mathbf{V}(0)+\\pi^{\\perp}_{\\mathbbm{1}}e_{n+1}(\\mathbf{L}z)^{\\top}=\\mathbf{W}(0)+v(\\mathbf{L}z)^{\\top}. (18) Here, \\displaystyle v=\\pi^{\\perp}_{\\mathbbm{1}}e_{n+1}=e_{n+1}-\\frac{1}{n+1}\\mathbbm{1}=\\frac{1}{n+1}(-1-1\\ldots-1\\ n)^{\\top},\\ \\ \\ \\text{with}\\ \\ \\ \\|v\\|^{2}=\\frac{n}{n+1}. (19) Equation ( [reference] ) shows that \\mathbf{W}(z) is a rank one perturbation of \\mathbf{W}(0) . This crucial property is exploited in Lemma [reference] , to prove our main Theorem [reference] . Remark (Time series framework) . In the context of time series [ [reference] , [reference] ] , U_{i} takes the form U_{i}=(U_{i}^{t})_{t\\in\\mathbb{N}} . Given a multi-horizon predictor (\\widehat{U}_{t+1}^{i},\\ldots,\\widehat{U}_{t+\\ell}^{i})=\\widehat{f}(U_{t-k+1}^{i},\\ldots,U_{t}^{i}) one then computes the time series of the residuals, R_{t}^{i}=U_{t}^{i}-\\widehat{U}_{t}^{i} .\nAt a given time t , we aim at localizing with high probability the next \\ell residuals, Y=(R_{t+1},\\dots,R_{t+\\ell}) using the k previous ones, X=(R_{t-k+1},\\dots,R_{t}) . It may also happen that the time series and so the residuals are both multidimensional ( R_{s}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d} ). This is e.g. the case for time series describing the evolution of a position in \\mathbb{R}^{3} ( d=3 ). In this case, X is a vector in \\mathbb{R}^{kd} and Y as a vector in \\mathbb{R}^{\\ell d} . The vector corresponding to the i\\textsuperscript{th} time series takes the form \\displaystyle V_{i}=(X_{i}^{\\top}\\ Y_{i}^{\\top})^{\\top}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{dk+d\\ell}. (20) This time series setting falls in the framework of this paper and will be developed extensively in [ [reference] ] . The most direct interpretation of the score vector \\text{diag}(\\mathbf{S}(z))=(S_{1}(z),\\ldots,S_{n+1}(z)) is the empirical Mahalanobis distance. This metric is e.g. also the central tool in linear discriminant analysis (LDA) [ [reference] ] , Chapter 9-10. This is closely related to the Karhunen-Loève decomposition, which consists in finding an orthonormal basis (\\xi_{i}\\otimes\\phi_{i})_{i\\in\\mathbb{N}}\\subset L^{2}(\\mathbb{P})\\otimes L^{2}(\\mathbb{R}^{d}) on which a random field is naturally decomposed.\nIt is worth to notice that other interpretations in terms of leverage score and shape theory can also be made. The interested reader will find some insights in Appendix [reference] .\nS3.SS3 3.3 The first ellipsoid \\mathcal{E}_{\\alpha}^{n} : main theorem We first need to introduce some notations.\nWhile the score \\mathbf{S}(z) is built on a procedure which is exchangeable in the (n+1) -sample, we will in fact need quantities that are obtained only using the n sample (V_{1},\\dots,V_{n}) . For this, we introduce the matrices \\mathbf{B}_{n}\\in\\mathcal{M}_{n,p} , \\widehat{\\mathbf{\\Sigma}}_{n,\\lambda}\\in\\mathcal{M}_{p,p} and \\mathbf{P}_{n,\\lambda}\\in\\mathcal{M}_{n,n} such that \\displaystyle(\\mathbf{B}_{n})_{ij} \\displaystyle\\coloneqq(V_{i})_{j}-\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{k=1}^{n}(V_{k})_{j},\\ \\ \\widehat{\\mathbf{\\Sigma}}_{n,\\lambda}\\coloneqq\\frac{1}{n}\\mathbf{B}_{n}^{\\top}\\mathbf{B}_{n}+\\lambda\\mathbf{I}_{p}=\\begin{pmatrix}\\widehat{\\mathbf{\\Sigma}}_{n,\\lambda}^{11}&\\widehat{\\mathbf{\\Sigma}}_{n}^{12}\\\\\n\\widehat{\\mathbf{\\Sigma}}_{n}^{21}&\\widehat{\\mathbf{\\Sigma}}_{n,\\lambda}^{22}\\end{pmatrix}, (21) \\displaystyle\\mathbf{P}_{n,\\lambda} \\displaystyle\\coloneqq\\mathbf{B}_{n}(\\mathbf{B}_{n}^{\\top}\\mathbf{B}_{n}+n\\lambda\\mathbf{I}_{p})^{-1}\\mathbf{B}_{n}^{\\top}=\\frac{1}{n}\\mathbf{B}_{n}\\widehat{\\mathbf{\\Sigma}}_{n,\\lambda}^{-1}\\mathbf{B}_{n}^{\\top}. (22) Above, \\widehat{\\mathbf{\\Sigma}}_{n,\\lambda}^{11}\\in\\mathcal{M}_{k,k},\\ \\widehat{\\mathbf{\\Sigma}}_{n,\\lambda}^{22}\\in\\mathcal{M}_{\\ell,\\ell},\\ \\widehat{\\mathbf{\\Sigma}}_{n}^{12}\\in\\mathcal{M}_{k,\\ell}, and \\widehat{\\mathbf{\\Sigma}}_{n}^{21}=(\\widehat{\\mathbf{\\Sigma}}_{n}^{12})^{\\top}\\in\\mathcal{M}_{\\ell,k} . \\mathbf{B}_{n} contains the data of the n -sample after being centered with their empirical mean, and \\widehat{\\mathbf{\\Sigma}}_{n,\\lambda} is the corresponding empirical ridge covariance matrix.\nThe matrix \\mathbf{P}_{n,\\lambda} is a regularized orthogonal projector, that is, \\mathbf{P}_{n,0} is an orthogonal projector, \\mathbf{P}_{n,\\lambda}^{\\top}=\\mathbf{P}_{n,\\lambda} and 0\\preccurlyeq\\mathbf{P}_{n,\\lambda}\\preccurlyeq\\mathbf{P}_{n,0} for \\lambda>0 . Denoting p_{i,n}=(\\mathbf{P}_{n,\\lambda})_{ii} ( p_{i,n}\\in[0,1] ) we set \\displaystyle q_{n,\\alpha}\\coloneqq np_{(n_{\\alpha})}, (23) the n^{\\text{th}}_{\\alpha} order statistic of the n -tuple (np_{1,n},\\dots,np_{n,n}) . Note that p_{(n_{\\alpha})} is the empirical quantile of order (1-\\alpha)(n+1)/n for the n -tuple (p_{1,n},\\dots,p_{n,n}) , and not 1-\\alpha (apply e.g. [ [reference] ] , p. 305). Contrarily to standard n -samples, the p_{i,n} are not independent. For example, they are constrained by the following deterministic inequality: \\sum_{i=1}^{n}p_{i,n}=\\mathrm{Tr}(\\mathbf{P}_{n,\\lambda})\\leq\\mathrm{Tr}(\\mathbf{P}_{n,0})=\\text{rank}(\\mathbf{P}_{n,0})\\leq p . We can now state our first main result. Theorem 3.1 . Let \\alpha\\in(1/(n+1),1) . let ((X_{1},Y_{1}),\\dots,(X_{n+1},Y_{n+1})) \\in(\\mathcal{X}\\times\\mathcal{Y})^{n+1} exchangeable pairs of random vectors. If q_{n,\\alpha}\\geq n-1 , we set \\mathcal{E}_{\\alpha}^{n}\\coloneqq\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell} . If q_{n,\\alpha}<n-1 , we define \\mathcal{E}_{\\alpha}^{n} as the following ellipsoid, \\displaystyle\\mathcal{E}_{\\alpha}^{n}=\\{z\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell}:(z-Z_{0}^{n})^{\\top}\\bm{\\mathcal{A}}_{n}^{-1}(z-Z_{0}^{n})\\leq\\rho_{n,\\alpha}\\}, (24) where, setting \\overline{X}_{n}\\coloneqq n^{-1}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}X_{i},\\ \\overline{R}_{n}\\coloneqq n^{-1}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}R_{i} and X_{n+1}^{\\mathrm{c}}=X_{n+1}-\\overline{X}_{n} , \\displaystyle\\bm{\\mathcal{A}}_{n} \\displaystyle=\\widehat{\\mathbf{\\Sigma}}_{n,\\lambda}/\\widehat{\\mathbf{\\Sigma}}_{n,\\lambda}^{11}, (25) \\displaystyle Z_{0}^{n} \\displaystyle=\\widehat{\\mathbf{\\Sigma}}_{n}^{21}(\\widehat{\\mathbf{\\Sigma}}_{n,\\lambda}^{11})^{-1}X_{n+1}^{\\mathrm{c}}+\\overline{R}_{n}, (26) \\displaystyle\\rho_{n,\\alpha} \\displaystyle=\\frac{q_{n,\\alpha}+1}{1-(q_{n,\\alpha}+1)/n}-1-(X_{n+1}^{\\mathrm{c}})^{\\top}(\\widehat{\\mathbf{\\Sigma}}_{n,\\lambda}^{11})^{-1}X_{n+1}^{\\mathrm{c}}. (27) Then the set \\mathcal{E}_{\\alpha}^{n} satisfies \\displaystyle\\mathbb{P}(Y_{n+1}-\\widehat{Y}_{n+1}\\in\\mathcal{E}_{\\alpha}^{n})\\geq 1-\\alpha. (28) The ellipsoid in Theorem [reference] is recovered by E_{\\alpha}^{n}=\\{\\widehat{Y}_{n+1}\\}+\\mathcal{E}_{\\alpha}^{n} , in the sense of the usual Minkowski set addition. An important feature of Lemma [reference] is that \\bm{\\mathcal{A}}_{n} does not depend on V_{n+1} . Likewise, \\rho_{n,\\alpha} and Z_{0}^{n} depend on V_{n+1} only through X_{n+1} . Interestingly, when the distribution of V_{1} is elliptical (see Section [reference] ) and V_{1} admits second order moment, the matrix \\bm{\\mathcal{A}}_{n} corresponds to an empirical estimate of the conditional covariance matrix \\mathrm{Cov}(R_{1}|X_{1}) , up to a random positive multiplicative constant (see [ [reference] ] , Theorem 1.5.4). In fact, one can see from equation ( [reference] ) that only \\rho_{n,\\alpha}\\bm{\\mathcal{A}}_{n} is uniquely defined. This allows us to interpret \\bm{\\mathcal{A}}_{n} as an empirical conditional covariance matrix for such distributions, up to a rescaling of \\rho_{n,\\alpha} with the said multiplicative constant. Remark (Empirical linear conditional expectation) . Observe that Z_{0}^{n}=\\widehat{e}_{n}+\\widehat{\\mathbf{A}}_{n}X_{n+1} , where \\displaystyle\\widehat{\\mathbf{A}}_{n}=\\widehat{\\mathbf{\\Sigma}}_{n}^{21}(\\widehat{\\mathbf{\\Sigma}}_{n,\\lambda}^{11})^{-1},\\ \\ \\widehat{e}_{n}=\\overline{R}_{n}-\\widehat{\\mathbf{A}}_{n}\\overline{X}_{n}. (29) Interestingly, it is easily shown that (\\widehat{e}_{n},\\widehat{\\mathbf{A}}_{n}) is an M-estimator obtained by minimizing \\displaystyle T_{n}(e,\\mathbf{A})=\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\|R_{i}-e-\\mathbf{A}X_{i}\\|^{2}+\\lambda\\|\\mathbf{A}\\|_{F}^{2}, (30) which simply corresponds to ridge multivariate linear regression. The continuous counterpart of T_{n} is T(e,\\mathbf{A})=\\mathbb{E}[\\|R-e-\\mathbf{A}X\\|^{2}]+\\lambda\\|\\mathbf{A}\\|_{F}^{2} , which is minimal for \\mathbf{A}_{*}=\\bm{\\Sigma}^{21}(\\bm{\\Sigma}_{\\lambda}^{11})^{-1} and e_{*}=\\mathbb{E}[R_{1}]-\\mathbf{A}_{*}\\mathbb{E}[X_{1}] . The pair (e_{*},A_{*}) yields the best affine approximation of R in terms of X , in the sense of L^{2}(\\mathbb{P}) , and the resulting affine map is known as the linear conditional expectation ( [ [reference] ] , e.g. Theorem 4.14). In comparison, the (full) conditional expectation is obtained by minimizing the map T(f)=\\mathbb{E}[\\|R-f(X)\\|^{2}] over all measurable maps f . Finally, \\bm{\\mathcal{A}}_{n} and (\\widehat{e}_{n},\\widehat{\\mathbf{A}}_{n}) are linked together by \\min T_{n}=T_{n}(\\widehat{e}_{n},\\widehat{\\mathbf{A}}_{n})=\\mathrm{Tr}(\\bm{\\mathcal{A}}_{n})-\\ell\\lambda . In the previous theorem, the introduced objects are not exchangeable in terms of the (n+1) -sample.\nNetherveless, we will show in next section how the ellipsoid \\mathcal{E}_{\\alpha}^{n} can be exchangeably rewritten.\nS3.SS5 3.5 The adjusted Mahalanobis score and the second ellipsoid \\mathcal{F}_{\\alpha}^{n} Following equation ( [reference] ), we can correct the score matrix ( [reference] ) to prevent empty confidence regions, by subtracting the \\mathbf{X} part of \\mathbf{S}(z) to \\mathbf{S}(z) . Explicitly, define \\mathbf{F}\\coloneqq\\pi_{\\mathbbm{1}}^{\\perp}\\mathbf{X}\\in\\mathcal{M}_{n+1,k} , where the i\\textsuperscript{th} row of \\mathbf{X} is X_{i}^{\\top} , and introduce \\displaystyle\\mathbf{S}^{\\prime}(z)\\coloneqq\\mathbf{S}(z)-\\mathbf{S}_{X},\\ \\ \\ \\mathbf{S}_{X}=\\mathbf{F}\\bigg(\\frac{1}{n}\\mathbf{F}^{\\top}\\mathbf{F}+\\lambda\\mathbf{I}_{k}\\bigg)^{-1}\\mathbf{F}^{\\top}. (42) As with \\mathbf{S}(z) , the associated score for V_{i} is S_{i}^{\\prime}(z)=\\mathbf{S}^{\\prime}(z)_{ii} and the associated conformal set is \\{z:S_{n+1}^{\\prime}(z)\\leq S_{(n_{\\alpha})}^{\\prime}(z)\\} , where S_{(n_{\\alpha})}^{\\prime}(z) is an order statistic built from (S_{1}^{\\prime}(z),\\ldots,S_{n}^{\\prime}(z)) .\nBecause \\mathbf{X} depends on X_{n+1} through a rank-one perturbation, namely \\mathbf{X}=(X_{1}\\ \\ldots\\ X_{n}\\ 0_{k,1})^{\\top}+e_{n+1}X_{n+1}^{\\top} ,\nwe can use Lemmas [reference] and [reference] to rewrite \\mathbf{S}_{X} as \\displaystyle\\frac{1}{n}\\mathbf{S}_{X}=\\begin{pmatrix}\\mathbf{P}_{n,\\lambda}^{XX}&\\mathbf{0}_{n,1}\\\\\n\\mathbf{0}_{1,n}&0\\end{pmatrix}+ww^{\\top}-\\frac{b_{X}^{n}(b_{X}^{n})^{\\top}}{1+d_{n}^{X}}, (43) for some matrix P_{n,\\lambda}^{XX}\\in\\mathcal{M}_{n,n} , some vector b_{X}^{n}\\in\\mathcal{M}_{n+1,1} and some d_{n}^{X}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{+} (see Lemma [reference] for expressions). Notably, \\displaystyle d_{n}^{X} \\displaystyle=\\frac{1}{n+1}(X_{n+1}^{\\mathrm{c}})^{\\top}(\\mathbf{\\Sigma}_{n,\\lambda}^{11})^{-1}X_{n+1}^{\\mathrm{c}},\\ \\ \\ X_{n+1}^{\\mathrm{c}}=X_{n+1}-\\overline{X}_{n}. The matrix \\mathbf{S}^{\\prime}(z) is finally written as \\displaystyle\\mathbf{S}^{\\prime}(z)=n\\begin{pmatrix}\\mathbf{P}_{n,\\lambda}-\\mathbf{P}_{n,\\lambda}^{XX}&\\mathbf{0}_{n,1}\\\\\n\\mathbf{0}_{1,n}&0\\end{pmatrix}-n\\frac{b^{n}(z)b^{n}(z)^{\\top}}{1+d_{n}(z)}+n\\frac{b_{X}^{n}(b_{X}^{n})^{\\top}}{1+d_{n}^{X}}. As in Lemma [reference] , we can discard the b^{n}(z) term in S_{i}^{\\prime}(z) , for i\\neq n+1 , leading to a second ellipsoidal set \\mathcal{F}_{\\alpha}^{n} . To describe this set, we finally need to introduce \\displaystyle p_{i,n}^{\\prime} \\displaystyle\\coloneqq(\\mathbf{P}_{n,\\lambda}-\\mathbf{P}_{n,\\lambda}^{XX})_{ii}+\\frac{(b_{X}^{n})_{i}^{2}}{1+d_{n}^{X}},\\ \\ \\ \\text{and}\\ \\ \\ q_{n,\\alpha}^{\\prime}\\coloneqq np_{(n_{\\alpha})}^{\\prime}, (44) where p_{(n_{\\alpha})}^{\\prime} is the order statistic of order n_{\\alpha} of (p_{1,n}^{\\prime},\\ldots,p_{n,n}^{\\prime}) .\nAs in Lemma [reference] , p_{i,n}^{\\prime} corresponds to \\mathbf{S}^{\\prime}(z)_{ii} after discarding the b^{n}(z)_{i} term. The analog of Theorem [reference] for the score S^{\\prime}(z) is the following, which describes a second ellipsoid \\mathcal{F}_{\\alpha}^{n}. Theorem 3.6 . Take the assumptions of Theorem [reference] , and define \\displaystyle t_{n}\\coloneqq\\bigg(\\frac{n+1}{n}\\bigg)(1+d_{n}^{X}). (45) If t_{n}q_{n,\\alpha}^{\\prime}\\geq n , we set \\mathcal{F}_{\\alpha}^{n}=\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell} . Else we set \\displaystyle\\mathcal{F}_{\\alpha}^{n}\\coloneqq\\{z\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell}:(z-Z_{0}^{n})^{\\top}\\bm{\\mathcal{A}}_{n}^{-1}(z-Z_{0}^{n})\\leq\\rho_{n,\\alpha}^{\\prime}\\}, (46) where Z_{0}^{n} and \\bm{\\mathcal{A}}_{n} are given in Theorem [reference] , and where \\displaystyle\\rho_{n,\\alpha}^{\\prime}\\coloneqq t_{n}^{2}\\frac{q_{n,\\alpha}^{\\prime}}{1-t_{n}q_{n,\\alpha}^{\\prime}/n}. (47) Then \\displaystyle\\mathbb{P}(Y_{n+1}-\\widehat{Y}_{n+1}\\in\\mathcal{F}_{\\alpha}^{n})\\geq 1-\\alpha. (48) As for E_{\\alpha}^{n} and \\mathcal{E}_{\\alpha}^{n} , the ellipsoid for Y_{n+1} is recovered by F_{\\alpha}^{n}=\\{\\widehat{Y}_{n+1}\\}+\\mathcal{F}_{\\alpha}^{n} .\nThe only difference between \\mathcal{E}_{\\alpha}^{n} and \\mathcal{F}_{\\alpha}^{n} is the parameter \\rho_{n,\\alpha}^{\\prime} .\nThe next proposition shows that \\mathcal{F}_{\\alpha}^{n} is never empty. However, there is a chance that \\mathcal{F}_{\\alpha}^{n}=\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell} : there is no equivalent of Proposition [reference] for \\mathcal{F}_{\\alpha}^{n} , except when n tends to \\infty . Proposition 3.7 . Under the assumptions of Theorem [reference] , \\rho_{n,\\alpha}^{\\prime}\\geq 0 . In particular, Z_{0}^{n}\\in\\mathcal{F}_{\\alpha}^{n} and \\mathcal{F}_{\\alpha}^{n} is never empty. Finally, under the assumptions of Proposition [reference] , \\displaystyle\\mathbb{P}(\\mathcal{F}_{\\alpha}^{n}=\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell})\\xlongrightarrow[n\\rightarrow\\infty]{}0. Remark (Behaviour of \\mathcal{E}_{\\alpha}^{n} and \\mathcal{F}_{\\alpha}^{n} w.r.t. large d_{n}^{X} ) . Observe that a large d_{n}^{X} means that X_{n+1} is far away from \\overline{X}_{n} , in the data metric \\mathbf{\\Sigma}_{n,\\lambda} . Interestingly, \\mathcal{E}_{\\alpha}^{n} and \\mathcal{F}_{\\alpha}^{n} have opposite behaviours with reference to large d_{n}^{X} . From equation ( [reference] ), large values of d_{n}^{X} correspond to small \\mathcal{E}_{\\alpha}^{n} ( \\mathcal{E}_{\\alpha}^{n}=\\varnothing in the worst case, see Proposition [reference] ), while they correspond to large \\mathcal{F}_{\\alpha}^{n} ( \\mathcal{F}_{\\alpha}^{n}=\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell} in the worst case, see Proposition [reference] ). In the first case, this is interpreted as X_{n+1} being so abnormal that no value of z can make the pair (X_{n+1}^{\\top}\\ z^{\\top})^{\\top} conform to the samples (V_{1},\\ldots,V_{n}) . In the second case, this is interpreted as the whole calibration sample (V_{1},\\ldots,V_{n}) being uninformative with reference to the current sample (X_{n+1}^{\\top}\\ R_{n+1}^{\\top})^{\\top} , when the new input X_{n+1} is too abnormal when compared to (X_{1},\\ldots,X_{n}) .\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
