{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '1,48p' build_claims.py; sed -n '110,155p' validate_artifacts.py; sed -n '75,145p' finalize_references.py; tail -28 REPRODUCIBILITY.md; cat .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh | head -30; ls -la /tmp/repo_support 2>/dev/null; sed -n '1,32p' paper.tex; sed -n '1,70p' figure_manifest.json; python - <<'PY'\nimport json\ns=json.load(open('paper_strategy.json'))\nfor k in ['central_thesis','method_groups','results_focus','figure_specs','notes_for_writer']: print(k, json.dumps(s[k],ensure_ascii=False)[:11000])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '1,48p' build_claims.py; sed -n '110,155p' validate_artifacts.py; sed -n '75,145p' finalize_references.py; tail -28 REPRODUCIBILITY.md; cat .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh | head -30; ls -la /tmp/repo_support 2>/dev/null; sed -n '1,32p' paper.tex; sed -n '1,70p' figure_manifest.json; python - <<'PY'\nimport json\ns=json.load(open('paper_strategy.json'))\nfor k in ['central_thesis','method_groups','results_focus','figure_specs','notes_for_writer']: print(k, json.dumps(s[k],ensure_ascii=False)[:11000])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"\"\"\"Claim inputs: @evidence[[paper_data.json]] and @evidence[[derived/summary.json]].\"\"\"\n\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\nDATA = json.loads((ROOT / 'paper_data.json').read_text())\nSUMMARY = json.loads((ROOT / 'derived/summary.json').read_text())\nEPISODES = 'final.evidence.episodes'\nCLAIMS = []\n\ndef claim(text, sources, citations=(), paths=()):\n    CLAIMS.append({'claim': text, 'source_ids': list(sources), 'citation_keys': list(citations),\n                   **({'implementation_evidence': list(paths)} if paths else {})})\n\ndef method_claim(text, paths, citations=()):\n    claim(text, [EPISODES, 'result.top_program_01'], citations, paths)\n\nfor case_index, name in enumerate(SUMMARY['labels'].values()):\n    entries = []\n    for method in ('euclidean', 'ilr', 'adaptive'):\n        coverage = SUMMARY[method]['coverage']['mean_by_condition'][case_index]\n        volume = SUMMARY[method]['volume']['mean_by_condition'][case_index]\n        entries.append(f'{method}: coverage={coverage:.9f}, volume={volume:.9f}')\n    claim(name + ' held-out condition means in the abstract, main text, Table 1 and Figure 2: ' + '; '.join(entries), [EPISODES])\n\nclaim('Adaptive volume is smaller than both references in every held-out heteroskedastic seed; this is an empirical configuration comparison, not population or component superiority.', [EPISODES])\nclaim('Abstract and all held-out plots use five seeds, six conditions, three methods, nominal coverage 0.90, and all retained seed observations; Figure 3 shows four overlapping descriptive sign strata in C2 and C4.', [EPISODES], paths=['source_evidence/evaluate_final.py:34-45', 'source_evidence/evaluate_final.py:924-928'])\nclaim('Every entry in the supplementary complete seed-level table, including all coverage Wilson intervals and volume Monte Carlo standard errors, is copied from released final episodes; complete_results.json preserves per-anchor rates and paired errors.', [EPISODES], paths=['build_artifacts.py', 'derived/complete_results.json', 'tables/seed_results.tex'])\n\nmetrics = DATA['final_evaluation']['metrics']\nfor metric in ('marginal_coverage', 'normalized_volume', 'coverage_gap_vs_euclidean', 'volume_gain_vs_euclidean', 'coverage_gap_vs_ilr', 'volume_gain_vs_ilr'):\n    claim(f'Equal-weight six-condition final {metric} = {metrics[metric]:.12f}. Coverage contrasts are adaptive minus reference; volume reduction is reference minus adaptive.', ['final.metric.' + metric, EPISODES])\n\nfor name, interval in zip(('coverage vs Euclidean', 'volume reduction vs Euclidean', 'coverage vs ILR', 'volume reduction vs ILR'), SUMMARY['paired_bootstrap_95']):\n    claim(f'Aggregate paired percentile bootstrap 95% interval for {name}: {interval}. Five seed blocks, 2000 resamples, all six conditions retained; not per-condition or simultaneous.', [EPISODES], ('Efron1979', 'Davison1997'), ('source_evidence/evaluate_final.py:1266-1294',))\n\nfor condition in ('C2', 'C4'):\n    for method in ('euclidean', 'ilr', 'adaptive'):\n        rows = []\n        for episode in DATA['final_evaluation']['evaluation_evidence']['episodes']:\n            record = next(case for case in episode['solution']['cases'] if case['case'] == condition)\n            result = record['details']['candidate'] if method == 'adaptive' else record['details']['baselines'][method]\n            rows.append(result['strata'])\n        means = [sum(row[index] for row in rows) / len(rows) for index in range(4)]\n        claim(f'{condition} {method} mean descriptive sign-stratum coverages in order x1<0, x1>=0, x2<0, x2>=0: {means}; no simultaneous conditional guarantee.', [EPISODES])\n\nclaim('The retained worst-stratum summary is 0.0; negative-sign masks in the constant-input control are empty and encoded as zero. It is not an observed zero-coverage subgroup; per-mask sample counts are absent from episode records.', ['final.metric.worst_stratum_coverage', EPISODES], paths=['source_evidence/evaluate_final.py:924-928', 'source_evidence/evaluate_final.py:1254-1263'])\n    if path.is_symlink():\n        raise ValueError('Source tree symlink: ' + str(path))\n    if path.is_dir():\n        digest.update(b'dir\\0' + relative + b'\\0')\n    else:\n        content = path.read_bytes()\n        digest.update(b'file\\0' + relative + b'\\0' + str(len(content)).encode() + b'\\0')\n        digest.update(content)\nidentity = {'selected_tree_sha256': 'sha256:' + digest.hexdigest(),\n            'sealed_tree_sha256': REGISTRY['final_evaluation']['program_tree_sha256'],\n            'evaluator_copy_sha256': 'sha256:' + hashlib.sha256((ROOT / 'source_evidence/evaluate_final.py').read_bytes()).hexdigest(),\n            'sealed_evaluator_sha256': REGISTRY['final_evaluation']['evaluator_sha256'],\n            'selected_source_copy_equal': (original / 'src/program.py').read_bytes() == (ROOT / 'source_evidence/adaptive.py').read_bytes(),\n            'implementation_support': 'Readable adaptive.py and evaluate_final.py, cited at line locations; matching hashes verify identity only.'}\nidentity['passed'] = identity['selected_tree_sha256'] == identity['sealed_tree_sha256'] and identity['evaluator_copy_sha256'] == identity['sealed_evaluator_sha256'] and identity['selected_source_copy_equal']\n(ROOT / 'source_evidence/identity_verification.json').write_text(json.dumps(identity, indent=2) + '\\n')\nREPORT['checks']['source_identity'] = identity\nif not identity['passed']:\n    REPORT['hard_errors'].append('Evaluated source identity mismatch')\n\nfor artifact_name, inputs in {\n    'paper.tex': ['paper_data.json', 'references_provenance.json', 'source_evidence/adaptive.py', 'source_evidence/evaluate_final.py'],\n    'paper.pdf': ['paper.tex', 'references.bib', 'figure_manifest.json', 'tables/condition_means.tex'],\n    'claims_grounding.json': ['paper.tex', 'paper_data.json', 'references_provenance.json', 'build_claims.py'],\n    'figure_manifest.json': ['build_artifacts.py', 'paper_strategy.json', 'paper_data.json'],\n    'references.bib': ['references_provenance.json', 'finalize_references.py'],\n    'references_provenance.json': ['literature/reading_report.txt', 'finalize_references.py'],\n    'reproduction/inventory.json': ['reproduction/resources/final/manifest.json', 'reproduction/resources/search/manifest.json', 'package_reproduction.py'],\n    'reproduction/run_provenance.json': ['paper_data.json', 'package_reproduction.py'],\n    'source_evidence/identity_verification.json': ['source_evidence/adaptive.py', 'source_evidence/evaluate_final.py', 'source_evidence/final_evaluation_manifest.json'],\n}.items():\n    artifact = ROOT / artifact_name\n    provenance = {'schema_name': 'scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest',\n        'artifact': {'path': artifact_name, 'kind': 'manuscript' if artifact_name in ('paper.tex', 'paper.pdf') else 'derived',\n                     'sha256': hashlib.sha256(artifact.read_bytes()).hexdigest()},\n        'derived_from': ['@' + 'evidence[[' + source + ']]' for source in inputs],\n        'generated_by': {'script_path': 'validate_artifacts.py'},\n        'notes': 'This sidecar records inspected manuscript-input provenance; it does not replace semantic claim review.'}\n    Path(str(artifact) + '.prov.json').write_text(json.dumps(provenance, indent=2) + '\\n')\n\nif run('claims', [sys.executable, ROOT / '.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/scripts/validate_claims.py', '--paper-data', 'paper_data.json', '--references', 'references_provenance.json', '--bib', 'references.bib', '--claims', 'claims_grounding.json', '--paper-dir', '.']).returncode:\n    REPORT['hard_errors'].append('claims failed')\n\nledger_entry = {'schema_name': 'scientific-evidence.ledger-entry', 'entry_id': 'paper-validation-' + NOW,\n    'entry_kind': 'verification', 'subject': {'path': 'paper.tex', 'kind': 'artifact'},\n    'evidence_refs': ['@evidence[[paper_data.json]]', '@evidence[[claims_grounding.json]]', '@evidence[[source_evidence/identity_verification.json]]'],\n        else:\n            entry_type = 'article'\n            fields['journal'] = tex(metadata.get('container-title', [''])[0])\n        for field in ('volume', 'issue', 'page'):\n            if metadata.get(field):\n                fields['pages' if field == 'page' else 'number' if field == 'issue' else field] = tex(metadata[field])\n        scope = 'Read bibliographic metadata and abstract where supplied. No full-text review or novelty certification claimed.'\n    reading = {'title': title, 'abstract': abstract or None, 'reading_scope': scope,\n               'source_url': url, 'no_citation_chaining': True}\n    (directory / 'reading.json').write_text(json.dumps(reading, indent=2) + '\\n')\n    bibliography.append('@' + entry_type + '{' + key + ',\\n' + '\\n'.join('  ' + field + ' = {' + str(value) + '},' for field, value in fields.items()) + '\\n}\\n')\n    records.append({'id': 'ref.' + key, 'paper_id': identifier, 'citation_key': key, 'title': title,\n                    'year': year, 'query': 'Direct identifier lookup: ' + identifier, 'source_url': url,\n                    'retrieved_at': NOW, 'reading_path': 'literature/' + key + '/reading.json', 'reading_scope': scope})\n    print(key, title, '\\n', abstract[:1000] if abstract else '[No abstract supplied: bibliographic/background citation only]', flush=True)\n\noriginal = ROOT / 'literature' / 'original_references.bib'\nif not original.exists():\n    original.write_bytes((ROOT / 'references.bib').read_bytes())\n(ROOT / 'references.bib').write_text('\\n'.join(bibliography))\n(ROOT / 'references_provenance.json').write_text(json.dumps({'generated_at': NOW, 'count': len(records),\n    'queries': [record['query'] for record in records], 'references': records}, indent=2) + '\\n')\nThe evaluator copies the source directory into an isolated process. For minimal replay packaging, copy `source_evidence/adaptive.py` alone into a clean temporary source directory and use that path if the full source-evidence directory exceeds the evaluator's file limits. Preserve the original final NPZ files with matching manifest hashes. This replay command is documented from the inspected CLI and is not asserted to have been rerun here. Seed labels, counts and all other settings are read from the frozen evaluator/resource manifests.\n\nObserved retained per-seed episode duration totals span 6.46–7.59 seconds. These are not full end-to-end timings, installation timings or hardware-specific forecasts. No new wall-time estimate is fabricated. Figure construction and literature lookup are separate from those measured durations.\n\n## Output map\n\n| Manuscript object | Construction | Inputs |\n|---|---|---|\n| Overview | Outer `generate-concept-figure` workflow | `paper_strategy.json`, finished `paper.tex` |\n| Condition comparison | `build_artifacts.py` | final episode coverage and volume |\n| Sign-stratum comparison | `build_artifacts.py` | C2/C4 final episode sign-mask rates |\n| Auxiliary configuration comparison | `build_artifacts.py` | three development configurations' episode records |\n| Condition-mean table | `build_artifacts.py` → `tables/condition_means.tex` | final episodes |\n| Complete seed table | `build_artifacts.py` → `tables/seed_results.tex` | final episodes including Wilson intervals and MC SE |\n| Aggregate contrast table | `paper.tex` | registered final metrics and released paired bootstrap intervals |\n| Operational-check table | `paper.tex` | final episode checks and independent qualification fields |\n\n`derived/complete_results.json` preserves per-anchor volumes, all sign-mask rates, radii/ranks, point errors and paired integration errors. Empty constant-input negative-sign masks keep their original zero encodings and are separately flagged as empty, never treated as measured zero coverage. `derived/summary.json` contains deterministic means and sample SDs across retained seeds, not newly simulated results. `derived/search_comparison.json` is strictly development-data evidence. All quantitative plots have PDF and 300-DPI PNG outputs, and LaTeX uses the vector PDF. Adjacent provenance sidecars point to actual source files.\n\n## Literature independence and reading scope\n\nNo broad task-identifying query or citation chaining was performed during manuscript writing. A supplied bibliography containing many application-specific compositional citations was replaced with direct-identifier lookups of foundational methodology. Original metadata, official arXiv abstracts and selected downloaded papers are under `literature/`; `reading.json`, `reading_report.txt`, and `references_provenance.json` state the actual scope of inspection. Selected conformal papers were inspected through their opening methodological pages; other entries were read at metadata/abstract level, not asserted to have undergone full-text review. Publisher access failures are retained rather than hidden. This is not a novelty certification or systematic review.\n\nNo prohibited-source encounter was identified during this manuscript-writing step. This statement does not certify unobserved earlier retrieval. If a later reader encounters prohibited material, stop reading it and append the URL, location and extent of exposure to the log rather than using it.\n\n## Verification\n\nUse the staged `validate-paper` scripts for source/citation checks, claim/provenance checks, figure schema/readability checks, compilation and visible Supplementary Information checks. Use the `scientific-evidence` verifier for dangling-reference checks. `validation_report.json` records the performed checks and any environmental limitation; it must not be interpreted as a substitute for a human scientific review. No private-entropy generation, new component ablation or missing gate diagnostic is inferred from a passing artifact check.\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\n\nSCRIPT_DIR=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")\" && pwd)\"\nexec python3 \"$SCRIPT_DIR/scientific_evidence.py\" \"$@\"\n\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n% --- end watermark ---\n\\usepackage{paper,times}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,booktabs,longtable,array,graphicx,subcaption}\n\\usepackage{hyperref,url}\n\\title{Adaptive ILR Ellipsoids: Coverage--Volume Trade-offs on the Simplex}\n\\author{Anonymous authors}\n\\newcommand{\\simplex}{\\Delta^{K-1}_{+}}\n\\newcommand{\\R}{\\mathbb{R}}\n\\newcommand{\\clip}{\\operatorname{clip}}\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nPrediction regions for continuous compositions must be evaluated both for joint coverage and for their size on the simplex. We study whether training-adaptive isometric log-ratio (ILR) ellipsoids offer a useful coverage--volume trade-off relative to Euclidean and isotropic ILR balls. The adaptive configuration estimates residual scales and boundary-dependent shapes using training data, while an independent split-conformal calibration determines the region threshold. Across five held-out seeds and six synthetic conditions, heteroskedastic logistic-normal responses yield mean coverage 0.9044 and simplex-volume fraction 0.1476, compared with 0.9030 and 0.3336 for Euclidean balls. The benefit is not uniform: boundary-concentrated Dirichlet responses require volume fraction 0.9759, while covariate-shifted coverage falls to 0.8633 against a 0.90 target. Descriptive sign-stratum coverage also remains uneven. These findings support condition-dependent evaluation of region geometry, not universal superiority or conditional validity. Common-measure integration and separate uncertainty summaries expose failures that a single aggregate objective would obscure.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json]]\n{\n  \"figures\": [\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"geometry_calibration_overview\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/geometry_calibration_overview.png\",\n      \"n_panels\": 1,\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        6\n      ],\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        3\n      ],\n      \"generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"prompt_log_path\": \"figures/geometry_calibration_overview.prompts.json\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 1,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 30,\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false,\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 8.5,\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"title\": 9,\n        \"axis\": 9,\n        \"ticks\": 8.5,\n        \"legend\": 8.5\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"coverage_volume_by_regime\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"scatter\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/coverage_volume_by_regime.png\",\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/coverage_volume_by_regime.pdf\",\n      \"n_panels\": 2,\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        6,\n        6\n      ],\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        3,\n        3\n      ],\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 1,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 30,\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false,\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 8.5,\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"title\": 9,\n        \"axis\": 9,\n        \"ticks\": 8.5,\n        \"legend\": 8.5\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"descriptive_sign_strata\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"scatter\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/descriptive_sign_strata.png\",\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/descriptive_sign_strata.pdf\",\n      \"n_panels\": 2,\ncentral_thesis \"In the tested synthetic regimes, externally calibrated adaptive ILR ellipsoids markedly reduce simplex-volume fractions under heteroskedastic logistic-normal responses, but nearly fill the simplex for near-boundary Dirichlet responses and remain below nominal coverage under covariate shift, so their empirical value is condition-dependent rather than uniformly superior.\"\nmethod_groups [{\"name\": \"Euclidean-simplex split-conformal balls\", \"role\": \"baseline\", \"include_reason\": \"Evaluator-owned ridge predictions in composition coordinates, clipped and renormalized, with Euclidean residual calibration and simplex-intersected membership; essential because adaptive ILR does not dominate this comparator in volume.\"}, {\"name\": \"Isotropic ILR split-conformal balls\", \"role\": \"baseline\", \"include_reason\": \"Evaluator-owned ridge locations in ILR coordinates with an unscaled residual norm; isolates a meaningful coordinate-space reference using the same external calibration and simplex-volume measure, without claiming a matched-training-size mechanism ablation.\"}, {\"name\": \"Four-fold residual adaptive ILR ellipsoids\", \"role\": \"proposed\", \"include_reason\": \"Frozen selected model and sole adaptive method with released final evidence; describe as the studied configuration rather than an established novel algorithm or universal winner.\"}, {\"name\": \"Single-split residual adaptive ILR ellipsoids\", \"role\": \"ablation\", \"include_reason\": \"Three search-seed measurements support a limited direct comparison with the four-fold configuration; the change bundles residual fitting, full-data location refitting, and training-data allocation, so it cannot identify a pure cross-fitting effect.\"}, {\"name\": \"Continuously interpolated covariance ellipsoids\", \"role\": \"ablation\", \"include_reason\": \"Three search-seed measurements test continuous covariance interpolation against hard routing while retaining four-fold residual fitting; show no established advantage and keep this auxiliary branch out of the central thesis.\"}]\nresults_focus [\"Primary final comparison: show nominal 90% joint coverage and normalized common-simplex volume separately for the frozen adaptive model and both baselines in all six conditions, with four regimes visually separated from the two matched controls.\", \"Heteroskedastic logistic-normal evidence: final five-seed means are coverage 0.904375 and volume 0.147566 for adaptive ILR, versus 0.903021/0.333621 for Euclidean and 0.903438/0.335896 for isotropic ILR; smaller measured sets at similar observed marginal coverage, not proven population dominance.\", \"Boundary and shift limitations belong in the main results: near-boundary volume is 0.975856 versus 0.715514 for Euclidean; shifted coverage is 0.863281 versus 0.859219 for Euclidean and 0.708177 for isotropic ILR. The adaptive model has larger shifted volume than Euclidean (0.622107 versus 0.192813), so coverage and efficiency must be interpreted jointly.\", \"Final equal-weight summary is coverage 0.894158 and volume 0.544618. Adaptive-minus-baseline coverage differences are -0.001441 for Euclidean and +0.025052 for isotropic ILR; baseline-minus-adaptive volume differences are -0.034631 and +0.049603 respectively. This average includes the two C2-derived controls and is not a broad population estimate.\", \"Use the released paired seed-bootstrap intervals only for the equal-weight six-condition contrast they estimate: coverage versus Euclidean [-0.006536, 0.003854], volume reduction versus Euclidean [-0.054606, -0.011514], coverage versus isotropic ILR [0.018516, 0.031884], and volume reduction versus isotropic ILR [0.030559, 0.061766]. They are exploratory five-seed intervals, not multiplicity-corrected generalization guarantees or per-condition intervals.\", \"Subgroup reliability: plot the evaluator’s four overlapping covariate-sign strata for heteroskedastic and shifted conditions. Training boundary tertiles and evaluation sign strata are different objects. Preserve the reported worst-stratum summary of 0.0 and explain that empty negative-sign strata in the constant-input control were encoded as zero; never call that an observed zero-coverage subgroup.\", \"Auxiliary search comparison: compare three configurations directly on common search seeds 101, 211, and 307, separate from final seeds 1009, 2027, 3049, 4051, and 5059. Show coverage and volume, not a best-so-far trace. No final evaluation exists for the two auxiliary configurations in the released bundle.\", \"Controls validate specific observed operations, not scientific superiority: constant covariates and training-pair derangement test information dependence; point-copy singleton and full-simplex checks anchor membership semantics; query-order invariance and replay check fixed predictions. Do not interpret their completion as conditional validity or real-data reliability.\"]\nfigure_specs [{\"figure_id\": \"geometry_calibration_overview\", \"required\": true, \"purpose\": \"Provide early glance value: strictly positive composition responses, three region geometries, training-only adaptive factors, independent calibration, and separate coverage/common-volume evaluation.\", \"figure_type\": \"diagram\", \"preferred_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\", \"group_by\": \"training_calibration_evaluation\", \"metric\": \"none_conceptual\", \"split_by\": \"information_partition\", \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\", \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0, \"layout_rationale\": \"The inspected ICLR style has one 5.5-inch text column. A shallow 5.5 by about 1.55-inch overview on page 1 or 2 gives three stages enough room for 9-point labels without turning the figure into a dense algorithm listing.\", \"allow_progression\": false, \"max_panels\": 1, \"max_panels_per_row\": 1, \"max_categories_per_panel\": 6, \"max_series_per_panel\": 3, \"intended_footprint\": \"Approximately 0.22 body page including caption; three stages within one cohesive panel, not three separate subfigures.\", \"content_budget\": \"At most six labeled nodes, three method-color keys, short stage titles, and no measured-looking toy scatter or invented region sizes. A simplex icon is conceptual, explicitly not experimental data.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"export_requirements\": \"Editable draw.io source and deterministic export; use a PDF if produced from source. The designated workflow may be raster-only, in which case regenerate at at least 2400 px wide, never upscale.\"}, {\"figure_id\": \"coverage_volume_by_regime\", \"required\": true, \"purpose\": \"Expose regime-specific benefits and failures without collapsing reliability and efficiency into one score.\", \"figure_type\": \"scatter\", \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\", \"group_by\": \"region_geometry\", \"metric\": \"joint_coverage_and_normalized_simplex_volume\", \"split_by\": \"synthetic_condition\", \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\", \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0, \"layout_rationale\": \"Use the full single-column 5.5-inch width, two stacked panels, and a common six-condition x-axis. Six short labels and three consistently colored series fit at 9-point final-size text; a half-width rendering would make method offsets, seed dots, and uncertainty unreadable.\", \"allow_progression\": false, \"max_panels\": 2, \"max_panels_per_row\": 1, \"max_categories_per_panel\": 6, \"max_series_per_panel\": 3, \"intended_footprint\": \"Approximately 5.5 by 3.4 inches plus caption, about 0.45 body page, near the start of main results.\", \"content_budget\": \"Three methods, five faint seed points and one mean marker per condition; no fourth decorative series. Use a dashed 0.90 line only in the coverage panel. Volume uses the unit-simplex fraction scale. Separate the two matched controls with a vertical rule, not a chronology arrow.\", \"uncertainty_display\": \"Show seed dispersion directly. Do not repurpose the released aggregate bootstrap intervals as condition-specific error bars; keep Wilson intervals and Monte Carlo errors in the detailed table or supplement.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\", \"final.metric.marginal_coverage\", \"final.metric.normalized_volume\"], \"export_requirements\": \"Reproducible plotting source must export PDF and PNG at 300 DPI; LaTeX must include the PDF whenever available.\"}, {\"figure_id\": \"descriptive_sign_strata\", \"required\": true, \"purpose\": \"Show why near-nominal marginal coverage need not imply local reliability, while preserving the distinction between descriptive diagnostics and coverage guarantees.\", \"figure_type\": \"scatter\", \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\", \"group_by\": \"region_geometry\", \"metric\": \"empirical_joint_coverage_in_covariate_sign_stratum\", \"split_by\": \"heteroskedastic_and_shifted_regimes\", \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\", \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0, \"layout_rationale\": \"Two approximately 2.6-inch panels share one full-width 5.5-inch figure and one legend. Four short sign labels per panel and 9-point text fit without shrinking. The two regimes are scientific contrasts, not a temporal sequence.\", \"allow_progression\": false, \"max_panels\": 2, \"max_panels_per_row\": 2, \"max_categories_per_panel\": 4, \"max_series_per_panel\": 3, \"intended_footprint\": \"Approximately 5.5 by 2.25 inches plus caption, about 0.32 body page, in reliability and failure analysis.\", \"content_budget\": \"Only the four masks x1<0, x1>=0, x2<0, x2>=0, three methods, common y-axis limits, and one nominal line. Masks overlap across covariates and are not a four-way partition. Training-defined boundary groups are not depicted as evaluated coverage groups.\", \"uncertainty_display\": \"Show all five final-seed stratum rates and their means without unsupported simultaneous intervals. Empty strata are missing observations, never zero-rate plotted points; preserve the original zero-valued aggregate separately in the main text.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\", \"final.metric.worst_stratum_coverage\"], \"export_requirements\": \"Export PDF and PNG at 300 DPI from plotting source; reference PDF from LaTeX.\"}, {\"figure_id\": \"search_configuration_comparison\", \"required\": false, \"purpose\": \"Document the limited measured comparison of residual fitting and shape routing without asserting an isolated mechanism or a final generalization result.\", \"figure_type\": \"scatter\", \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\", \"group_by\": \"training_configuration\", \"metric\": \"joint_coverage_and_normalized_simplex_volume\", \"split_by\": \"search_condition\", \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\", \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0, \"layout_rationale\": \"Supplementary single-column figure uses the same 5.5-inch two-stacked-panel design as the main comparison, preserving method labels and raw three-seed dispersion.\", \"allow_progression\": false, \"max_panels\": 2, \"max_panels_per_row\": 1, \"max_categories_per_panel\": 6, \"max_series_per_panel\": 3, \"intended_footprint\": \"About half a supplementary page; include only if a compact direct-comparison table does not already communicate the same evidence.\", \"source_ids\": [\"result.top_program_01\", \"result.top_program_02\", \"result.top_program_03\"], \"export_requirements\": \"Use search episodes under all_programs, not the final episodes; export PDF and 300-DPI PNG from source and include PDF.\"}]\nnotes_for_writer [\"Write a standalone empirical study, not a final manuscript at this strategy stage. No paper.tex, claims_grounding.json, new experiment, rendered figure, external retrieval, or final-paper validation is authorized here. A later writer must use the standalone skill with the venue constraints taking precedence over generic template defaults.\", \"Body plan targets 8.5 single-column pages, within the 8–9 page requirement: abstract/introduction 1.10; focused related work 0.65; prediction target and measurement rationale 0.90; method and assumptions 1.45; experimental protocol 0.90; primary comparisons 1.35; reliability, failure analysis, and compact configuration comparison 1.20; discussion/limitations/conclusion 0.95. These are planning allocations, not measured page counts; figures and captions are included, references and supplement are excluded. If ending at eight pages, page eight must be at least 75% full.\", \"Use substantive depth to fill the format: distinguish random continuous composition responses from class labels and unidentified latent probabilities; explain why ILR and Euclidean volumes are measured in the same simplex measure; give the training/calibration information boundary; show per-condition reversals and all key failure caveats. Do not pad with a search diary, broad unrelated related work, large whitespace, redundant plots, or invented ablations.\", \"Section order: Introduction; Related Work; Prediction Target and Evaluation; Training-Adaptive ILR Regions; Experimental Protocol; Coverage–Volume Trade-offs; Reliability and Failure Modes; Discussion and Limitations; Conclusion. After references, include Supplementary Information and the required separate LLM Usage section. Keep essential limitations, final status, and uncertainty in the main body.\", \"Layout is grounded in paper.sty lines 48–49: one text column, 5.5 inches wide and 9 inches high. Here full_width and column width coincide; full_width means the entire text block, not a two-column float. Use approximately 9-point minimum final figure labels, one consistent method-color palette, and no more than three panels per row. Do not shrink a dense panel to rescue layout.\", \"Only one overview uses generate-concept-figure; plan its early location, six-node content budget, editable source, and prompt provenance. It is a conceptual schematic, not a data figure or invented measured simplex illustration. The later writer wires this generator according to the standalone workflow; this strategy stage does not invoke rendering.\", \"Every quantitative plot must be reproducible from the released JSON and export PDF plus 300-DPI PNG; LaTeX uses PDF when available. Raster-only overview output must be regenerated at least 2400 pixels wide for this full-width plan. Any later column-width raster must be at least 1200 pixels wide. Never upscale an existing image.\", \"Private-only grounding context: final.qualification.status=completed, final.qualification.outcome=qualified, and final.qualification.correct=true. Record the evaluator’s completed qualification honestly, but translate to a modest reader-facing audit statement rather than an accuracy/acceptance badge. No failed final qualification occurred in these artifacts; preserve any failure in future revisions if the authoritative status changes, without guessing its statistical, validity, or adaptivity cause.\", \"Private-only search provenance: result.total_attempts=3 and result.total_evaluated_programs=3, each with three released scored episodes; do not infer additional attempts or measured losses from missing records. Search ordering selects the four-fold hard-routing configuration (result.top_program_01); its internal selection utility is 0.8818756857036072 versus 0.8776261506257234 for the single-split configuration and 0.8818118342646846 for interpolation. These scores are not final evidence and need not appear visibly; if ever required, describe the explicitly defined coverage-volume utility, not a pipeline field name.\", \"There is no downstream_study, hence no study.* score-difference or attempt-status source. Do not import the bootstrap incumbent score from memory. The evaluator-owned Euclidean and isotropic ILR comparators remain the valid scientific baselines; the original starter is source-only unless additional authoritative measurements are supplied.\", \"Exact final evidence path in paper_data: final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes[*].solution.cases[*].details contains candidate and baselines.{euclidean,ilr}; shared intervals are solution.paired_seed_bootstrap_comparisons95, repeated across episodes but representing one bootstrap analysis, not five independent intervals. The four interval entries are coverage difference versus Euclidean, volume reduction versus Euclidean, coverage difference versus isotropic ILR, and volume reduction versus isotropic ILR.\", \"The main experiment has 4096 training, 2048 calibration, and 3840 test rows per final condition; the independent calibration quantile rank is 1845 at alpha=0.1. Baseline locations use the first 75% of training rows while the selected model refits on all training rows. Therefore the primary comparison is between end-to-end configurations, not an isolated covariance geometry intervention or a training-size-matched causal test.\", \"Expected theorem scope only: frozen training-derived scores plus independent exchangeable calibration/test pairs permit the ordinary split-conformal marginal guarantee for future continuous compositions. Explain the standard rank argument without claiming a new theorem. The shifted condition is not exchangeable with calibration; external calibration does not guarantee target-domain or conditional coverage. Do not label this model jackknife+, CV+, or conformalized group calibration.\", \"Condition-specific numbers in this strategy are deterministic averages of the five released final seeds, rounded to six decimals, not new experiments. Show individual seeds in figures and clarify what each uncertainty quantity conditions on. No per-condition bootstrap interval is released; do not create one by relabeling the aggregate interval.\", \"The reported worst-stratum summary 0.0 is an empty-mask encoding artifact in the constant-input control, not evidence of zero conditional coverage. The evaluator does not retain per-mask sample counts or test covariates in the episode JSON. A count-based reanalysis requires the available resource arrays and the exact intervention; do not invent denominators or silently replace the registered statistic. Any recomputed nonempty-mask summary must be explicitly new derived analysis and retain the original.\", \"The held-out evaluation reduces direct selection reuse, but five seeds and a small synthetic design do not establish a broad nonadaptivity audit, robustness certification, or real-data generalization. Candidate self-diagnostics are explicitly unverified; use evaluator-owned measured fields and documented checks, not speculative candidate reports.\", \"The final resource generator uses private entropy and stored arrays; public seed integers alone are insufficient to reconstruct those draws. Reproduction must preserve the distributed NPZ resources and manifest hashes or state this limitation. Do not claim an exact regenerate-from-seed pipeline that the artifacts do not support.\", \"Current references_provenance.json has 25 metadata-grounded entries, including ellipsoidal conformal work but many off-topic compositional applications. Do not cite all entries or claim to have read their full texts. Resolve permitted foundational split-conformal and ILR background through conservative allowed-title lookup and actual source reading before drafting; no invented keys, no task-identifying search or citation chaining, and stop/log any prohibited encounter. The present strategy is not a novelty certification.\", \"Use the anonymous ICLR 2026 venue brief: omit names, affiliations, acknowledgments, funding, and identifying links. The seeded template’s author placeholder and final-copy command are not permission to deanonymize. Keep its required watermark while anonymizing the submission; make the actual LLM roles explicit in the separate disclosure section.\", \"Interpret other framings explicitly: preserving the broad proposal would claim unimplemented methods; broadening to all conformal geometry families would lack evaluated comparisons; a pure cross-fitting improvement story would overattribute bundled changes; a boundary-robustness story would contradict near-full-simplex volume and shifted undercoverage. The chosen shared thesis across regime-specific evidence is stronger than each alternative.\", \"Source IDs identify available evidence categories; method formulas and generator details additionally require direct code references to results/ideas/02_cross_fitted_ilr_residual_geometry/program_1/src/program.py, task/evaluate_final.py, and task/resources/final/manifest.json. Auxiliary seed-level comparisons live in all_programs[*].evaluation_evidence, not in scalar ranking entries alone. Do not pretend a metric source proves a code-level mechanism.\", \"No later posthoc ranking is known at this stage. Route future authoritative appendix-ranked material to the supplement without retroactively changing this plan or promoting unsupported analyses; keep all evidence essential to the thesis in the main paper.\"]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
