{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '65,110p' scientific-evidence/schemas/artifact-manifest.schema.json; cat references_provenance.json | head -35; sed -n '1,60p' REPRODUCIBILITY.md; tail -5 literature/reading_report.txt; sed -n '17,60p' .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"          \"type\": \"array\"\n        },\n        \"cwd\": {\n          \"minLength\": 1,\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"finished_at\": {\n          \"format\": \"date-time\",\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"script_path\": {\n          \"minLength\": 1,\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"script_sha256\": {\n          \"pattern\": \"^[0-9a-f]{64}$\",\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"started_at\": {\n          \"format\": \"date-time\",\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        }\n      },\n      \"required\": [\n        \"script_path\"\n      ],\n      \"type\": \"object\"\n    },\n    \"named_outputs\": {\n      \"additionalProperties\": {\n        \"type\": [\n          \"string\",\n          \"number\",\n          \"integer\",\n          \"boolean\",\n          \"array\",\n          \"object\",\n          \"null\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"description\": \"Optional machine-readable outputs emitted by the derivation.\",\n      \"type\": \"object\"\n    },\n    \"notes\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    },\n{\n  \"campaign_results_dir\": \"work/results/r02/codex/20261107_044437/investigation\",\n  \"count\": 25,\n  \"generated_at\": \"2026-11-07T06:32:13.622671+00:00\",\n  \"queries\": [\n    \"Direct identifier lookup: 10.1007/b106715\",\n    \"Direct identifier lookup: 10.1007/3-540-36755-1_29\",\n    \"Direct identifier lookup: 10.1080/01621459.2017.1307116\",\n    \"Direct identifier lookup: 10.1214/20-AOS1965\",\n    \"Direct identifier lookup: arxiv:1904.06019\",\n    \"Direct identifier lookup: arxiv:2107.07511\",\n    \"Direct identifier lookup: 10.1111/j.2517-6161.1982.tb01195.x\",\n    \"Direct identifier lookup: 10.1023/A:1023818214614\",\n    \"Direct identifier lookup: 10.1093/biomet/67.2.261\",\n    \"Direct identifier lookup: 10.1002/9781119976462\",\n    \"Direct identifier lookup: 10.2202/1544-6115.1175\",\n    \"Direct identifier lookup: 10.1109/TSP.2010.2053029\",\n    \"Direct identifier lookup: 10.1080/00401706.1970.10488634\",\n    \"Direct identifier lookup: 10.1111/j.2517-6161.1974.tb00994.x\",\n    \"Direct identifier lookup: 10.1080/01621459.1975.10479865\",\n    \"Direct identifier lookup: 10.1214/ss/1009213286\",\n    \"Direct identifier lookup: 10.1214/aos/1176344552\",\n    \"Direct identifier lookup: 10.1017/CBO9780511802843\",\n    \"Direct identifier lookup: arxiv:1905.03222\",\n    \"Direct identifier lookup: 10.1093/imaiai/iaaa017\",\n    \"Direct identifier lookup: 10.1214/23-AOS2276\",\n    \"Direct identifier lookup: 10.1198/016214506000001437\",\n    \"Direct identifier lookup: arxiv:1209.2673\",\n    \"Direct identifier lookup: arxiv:2006.02544\",\n    \"Direct identifier lookup: arxiv:2306.09335\"\n  ],\n  \"reference_query_plan\": {},\n  \"reference_query_plans\": [],\n  \"references\": [\n    {\n# Reproduction and evidence guide\n\n## Scope and files\n\nThis manuscript analyzes retained evaluator outputs; manuscript preparation did not run a new model evaluation. `paper_data.json` is the read-only numerical registry. `claims_grounding.json` maps manuscript claims to its stable source identifiers and to bibliographic provenance. Additional `implementation_evidence` entries identify readable source locations, not merely hashes. `source_evidence/manifest.json` records the exact copied source files and hashes. The final evaluation is completed and qualified, but the scientific coverage/volume failures remain in the main paper.\n\nThe study is entirely synthetic. There is no downloaded real dataset, account requirement, or third-party dataset-license acceptance. Frozen resource files and their generation details are copied into `reproduction/resources/final/` and `reproduction/resources/search/`. `reproduction/inventory.json` records checked hashes; `package_reproduction.py` makes these exact source copies without running an evaluation. The original `task` link is not required to replay the packaged final corpus. Do not treat public seed labels as sufficient to regenerate the corpus: private per-resource entropy is used and the frozen NPZ arrays are necessary for exact replay.\n\n## Environment\n\nThe evaluated scientific dependency is `numpy==2.2.6`, as recorded in `source_evidence/requirements.txt`. The evaluated implementations are Python and standard-library code plus NumPy. The evaluator additionally requires Linux CPU-affinity/resource limits and its Landlock/seccomp isolation facilities. The original historical Python patch version and exact hardware were not retained in the evidence registry; manuscript construction uses the environment recorded in `paper_environment.json`, without claiming it was the historical evaluation environment.\n\nFor figures/tables and PDF inspection, install packages from `requirements-paper.txt` into a private environment outside this manuscript directory. A working TeX Live installation, BibTeX, and Poppler `pdftotext` are needed for compilation/validation. Do not put package trees in the manuscript workspace.\n\n```sh\npython3 -m venv /tmp/composition-paper-env\n/tmp/composition-paper-env/bin/pip install -r requirements-paper.txt\n/tmp/composition-paper-env/bin/python build_artifacts.py\n/tmp/composition-paper-env/bin/python build_claims.py\npdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error paper.tex\nbibtex paper\npdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error paper.tex\npdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error paper.tex\n```\n\nThe plotting script preserves an orchestrator-rendered overview when `.concept_figure_complete` exists or a nonempty overview prompt log is present. It never overwrites that rendered asset. Before orchestration the overview PNG is a compile-safe pending placeholder; the finished overview is supplied synchronously by the outer workflow, not claimed as a finished diagram by this writing step.\n\n## Scientific replay (not performed during writing)\n\nUse the distributed final evaluator and selected source, not a rewritten approximation:\n\n```sh\npython3 -m venv /tmp/composition-eval-env\n/tmp/composition-eval-env/bin/pip install -r source_evidence/requirements.txt\nTASK_SOURCE_DIR=\"$PWD/reproduction\" /tmp/composition-eval-env/bin/python source_evidence/evaluate_final.py \\\n  --program_path \"$PWD/source_evidence/adaptive.py\" \\\n  --results_dir /tmp/composition-replay\n```\n\nThe evaluator copies the source directory into an isolated process. For minimal replay packaging, copy `source_evidence/adaptive.py` alone into a clean temporary source directory and use that path if the full source-evidence directory exceeds the evaluator's file limits. Preserve the original final NPZ files with matching manifest hashes. This replay command is documented from the inspected CLI and is not asserted to have been rerun here. Seed labels, counts and all other settings are read from the frozen evaluator/resource manifests.\n\nObserved retained per-seed episode duration totals span 6.46–7.59 seconds. These are not full end-to-end timings, installation timings or hardware-specific forecasts. No new wall-time estimate is fabricated. Figure construction and literature lookup are separate from those measured durations.\n\n## Output map\n\n| Manuscript object | Construction | Inputs |\n|---|---|---|\n| Overview | Outer `generate-concept-figure` workflow | `paper_strategy.json`, finished `paper.tex` |\n| Condition comparison | `build_artifacts.py` | final episode coverage and volume |\n| Sign-stratum comparison | `build_artifacts.py` | C2/C4 final episode sign-mask rates |\n| Auxiliary configuration comparison | `build_artifacts.py` | three development configurations' episode records |\n| Condition-mean table | `build_artifacts.py` → `tables/condition_means.tex` | final episodes |\n| Complete seed table | `build_artifacts.py` → `tables/seed_results.tex` | final episodes including Wilson intervals and MC SE |\n| Aggregate contrast table | `paper.tex` | registered final metrics and released paired bootstrap intervals |\n| Operational-check table | `paper.tex` | final episode checks and independent qualification fields |\n\n`derived/complete_results.json` preserves per-anchor volumes, all sign-mask rates, radii/ranks, point errors and paired integration errors. Empty constant-input negative-sign masks keep their original zero encodings and are separately flagged as empty, never treated as measured zero coverage. `derived/summary.json` contains deterministic means and sample SDs across retained seeds, not newly simulated results. `derived/search_comparison.json` is strictly development-data evidence. All quantitative plots have PDF and 300-DPI PNG outputs, and LaTeX uses the vector PDF. Adjacent provenance sidecars point to actual source files.\n\n## Literature independence and reading scope\n\n Abstract: Conformal predictors are set predictors that are automatically valid in the sense of having coverage probability equal to or exceeding a given confidence level. Inductive conformal predictors are a computationally efficient version of conformal predictors satisfying the same property of validity. However, inductive conformal predictors have been only known to control unconditional coverage probability. This paper explores various versions of conditional validity and various ways to achieve them using inductive conformal predictors and their modifications.\nRomano2020 Classification with Valid and Adaptive Coverage \n Abstract: Conformal inference, cross-validation+, and the jackknife+ are hold-out methods that can be combined with virtually any machine learning algorithm to construct prediction sets with guaranteed marginal coverage. In this paper, we develop specialized versions of these techniques for categorical and unordered response labels that, in addition to providing marginal coverage, are also fully adaptive to complex data distributions, in the sense that they perform favorably in terms of approximate conditional coverage compared to alternative methods. The heart of our contribution is a novel conformity score, which we explicitly demonstrate to be powerful and intuitive for classification problems, but whose underlying principle is potentially far more general. Experiments on synthetic and real data demonstrate the practical value of our theoretical guarantees, as well as the statistical advantages of the proposed methods over the existing alternatives.\nDing2023 Class-Conditional Conformal Prediction with Many Classes \n Abstract: Standard conformal prediction methods provide a marginal coverage guarantee, which means that for a random test point, the conformal prediction set contains the true label with a user-specified probability. In many classification problems, we would like to obtain a stronger guarantee--that for test points of a specific class, the prediction set contains the true label with the same user-chosen probability. For the latter goal, existing conformal prediction methods do not work well when there is a limited amount of labeled data per class, as is often the case in real applications where the number of classes is large. We propose a method called clustered conformal prediction that clusters together classes having \"similar\" conformal scores and performs conformal prediction at the cluster level. Based on empirical evaluation across four image data sets with many (up to 1000) classes, we find that clustered conformal typically outperforms existing methods in terms of class-conditi\n- `paper_data.json`\n- `references.bib`\n- `references_provenance.json`\n- `contributions.md` (if provided) — optional base for framing the claimed contributions; not a grounding source\n- `global_memory_summary.md` (if provided)\n- Aggregated CSVs in `/` for fine-grained analysis (install and use pandas) — when available\n- generated figures in `figures/`\n- when `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also follow `presentation_contract` as a visible-language contract\n2. Use `templates/iclr/paper.tex` to keep section order stable. The paper format is **iclr**.\nInspect the selected template and any venue guidance before drafting so the author block, section structure, and framing expectations match the chosen format.\n3. Before drafting, choose one central thesis that is strongly supported by grounded evidence. Do not choose the paper story by recency alone.\n4. Group related implementations into a small number of named method families. Keep only the baseline, proposed method, and ablations needed to support the central thesis.\n5. If the artifacts do not support one coherent paper story, fail clearly instead of narrating the full search process.\n6. Write required sections:\n- abstract\n- introduction\n- related work\n- method\n- results\n- conclusion\n- references\nThe Supplementary Information section is mandatory.\nEmbed it in the same `paper.tex`, after the references, in this order: `\\clearpage`, bibliography, `\\clearpage`, `\\appendix`, and `\\section{Supplementary Information}`.\nReplace the template scaffold with substantive grounded content, including at least one meaningful subsection, table, or figure supported by the available artifacts. Suitable material includes extended methods, reproducibility details, additional ablations, robustness or failure analyses, and extra grounded tables or figures. Never move evidence essential to the central claim out of the main paper.\nRoute `appendix`-ranked posthoc analyses to Supplementary Information by default. Do not promote or drop them merely because of the main-body page budget.\n7. Generate comparative figures with Python + matplotlib by constructing analysis code yourself during the run:\n- inline `python - <<'PY' ... PY`, or\n- ad-hoc scripts in the paper workspace (for reproducibility).\n8. Prefer comparison figures over chronology figures. Use progression-style plots only when they directly support the main scientific claim.\n9. When multiple benchmarks, datasets, or settings are included, organize the visible paper around a shared hypothesis and condition-specific adaptations or limits, not around campaign chronology.\n10. If `paper_strategy.json` includes `figure_specs`, implement those figures first and keep every generated figure within the declared readability budgets.\n11. Use a logically consistent color scheme across all figures in the paper. Reuse the same colors for the same methods, baselines, conditions, or semantic roles whenever they recur, and avoid remapping meanings between figures without a clear reason.\n12. When a `figure_specs[*]` entry sets `preferred_generator` to `generate-concept-figure`, your job is to wire it in, not to render it. Do not invoke `autoillustrate` or the `generate-concept-figure` pipeline. Reference `./figures/<figure_id>.png` in the manuscript, create a lightweight placeholder PNG and `{}` prompt-log stub so the draft compiles, and register `generator: generate-concept-figure` plus `prompt_log_path: figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json` in `figure_manifest.json`. The orchestrator replaces those placeholders synchronously after the draft stage. Any reusable placeholder script must skip generation when `.concept_figure_complete` exists so repair rounds cannot overwrite the rendered asset.\n13. Do not satisfy a required overview-style `diagram` figure with a hand-built matplotlib/TikZ schematic unless the concept-figure workflow failed or was impossible; if that fallback happens, record the reason in `./memory.md`, `./global_memory/memory.md`, and the matching `figure_manifest.json` entry.\n14. Write `figure_manifest.json` for every figure referenced in `paper.tex`. Use `../validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json` as the canonical contract. Legacy flat rows and richer nested rows are both acceptable if they validate against that schema.\n15. Record generator provenance in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `generator`, `prompt_log_path`, and `fallback_reason` when a preferred generator was skipped.\nWhen evidence-audit feedback requires changing an orchestrator-owned concept figure, turn the correction into a complete documented fallback: export both `./figures/<figure_id>.png` and `./figures/<figure_id>.pdf`, set `generator` to the actual fallback generator (`matplotlib`, `tikz`, `native_latex`, or `manual`), set `attempted_generator: generate-concept-figure`, retain the original `prompt_log_path`, and set a non-empty `fallback_reason` describing why the orchestrator render was replaced. Use exactly those canonical artifact paths, and do not create or modify `./figures/<figure_id>.png.prov.json`; the orchestrator issues request-bound provenance after accepting the repair. Do not patch only the PNG while leaving `generator: generate-concept-figure`; that falsely claims ownership and allows figure preparation to restore the old render. Do not alter `paper_strategy.json` solely to bypass concept-figure orchestration, and do not create, delete, or modify `.concept_figure_complete`; the orchestrator owns that marker.\n16. Before choosing figure widths, inspect the selected paper template or style files to understand the practical column width, full-width span, and text footprint for the current paper format.\n17. Record final-layout hints in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `placement`, `intended_latex_width_fraction`, and `estimated_min_rendered_font_pt`.\n18. Keep figures readable by default: max 4 total panels, max 3 panels per row, max 6 categories per panel, max 4 series per panel, readable fonts, and abbreviated labels instead of tiny text unless the strategy explicitly allows a denser layout.\n19. Choose each LaTeX figure width from the actual generated figure dimensions, aspect ratio, the inspected template geometry, and intended paper footprint. If a figure becomes unreadable at column width, regenerate/simplify it or place it full-width instead of shrinking the same dense figure further.\n20. If `validation_feedback.json` or `validation_feedback.md` is present, treat it as a repair pass: fix every listed hard error first, rerun validation, and keep iterating until the hard errors are gone or the grounded inputs are insufficient.\n21. Do not create visible section or subsection titles centered on `milestones`, `progression`, `timeline`, `phase`, `stage`, `round`, `Experiment A`, `Experiment B`, `first success`, or similar chronology-shaped labels unless the central claim is explicitly about dynamics.\n22. Do not use phrases like `first valid result`, `subsequent improvements`, `next-best`, `later variants`, or `we then tried` in the visible paper unless that wording is strictly necessary inside a brief protocol description.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
